var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"index.html#Tracking.jl","page":"Tracking.jl","text":"Tracking.jl tracks GNSS satellites as part of a receiver in two blocks:\n\ndownconversion and correlation, with SIMD-optimized CPU kernels;\ncode and carrier estimation, closing the loop (by default FLL-assisted PLL and DLL).\n\nBoth can be replaced through multiple dispatch.\n\nCorrelation runs over whole code blocks: N primary code periods (e.g. N·1023 chips for GPS L1 C/A), with N = 1 until the bit edge or secondary code is found. Antenna-array samples are correlated with one replica, preserving their phase relationships. One satellite can be tracked on several signals at once (e.g. GPS L1 C/A, L1C-D and L1C-P), sharing the carrier downconvert, with a correlator per signal.\n\nThe per-record loop arithmetic (correlators, discriminators, bit buffer, C/N₀ and Doppler estimators) lives in TrackingLoops.jl, so a hardware correlator's loop can run the same code. Tracking.jl re-exports the functions that read results: estimate_cn0, get_prompt, get_soft_bits, has_bit_or_secondary_code_been_found and a few more. Load TrackingLoops to configure a correlator, a C/N₀ or noise estimator, a Doppler estimator or a post-correlation filter, or for vector tracking; see the TrackingLoops.jl manual.\n\nusing Tracking, TrackingLoops, GNSSSignals","title":"Tracking.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"index.html#Supported-signals","page":"Tracking.jl","text":"Tracking.jl tracks every concrete signal type GNSSSignals.jl defines: GPS L1 C/A, L1C, L2C and L5, Galileo E1, E5a (including the E5a-QP acquisition aid), E5b and E6, and BeiDou B1I, B3I, B1C, B2a and B2b. The signal support matrix lists each one's integration length, sync feature, navigation data, PRNs and known limitations.\n\nPages = [\n  \"signals.md\",\n  \"track.md\",\n  \"tracking_state.md\",\n  \"bit_sync.md\",\n  \"loop_filter.md\",\n  \"custom_doppler_estimator.md\",\n  \"correlator.md\",\n  \"cn0_estimator.md\"\n]\nDepth = 1","title":"Supported signals"},{"category":"section","location":"index.html#Installation","page":"Tracking.jl","text":"julia> ]\npkg> add Tracking","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"index.html#Quick-start","page":"Tracking.jl","text":"The minimum to track a single GPS L1 C/A satellite: build a TrackState, seed it from an acquisition handoff via add_satellite!, then call track on each measurement.\n\njulia> using Tracking, GNSSSignals\n\njulia> using Tracking: Hz\n\njulia> using GNSSSignals: gen_code, get_code_frequency, get_code_center_frequency_ratio\n\njulia> track_state = TrackState(; signal = GPSL1CA());\n\njulia> track_state = add_satellite!(track_state; prn = 1, code_phase = 50.0, carrier_doppler = 1000.0Hz);\n\njulia> sampling_frequency = 4e6Hz;\n\njulia> num_samples = 4000;\n\njulia> signal = GPSL1CA();\n\njulia> code_frequency = 1000.0Hz * get_code_center_frequency_ratio(signal) + get_code_frequency(signal);\n\njulia> measurement = cis.(2π .* 1000.0Hz .* (0:num_samples-1) ./ sampling_frequency) .*\n           gen_code(num_samples, signal, 1, sampling_frequency, code_frequency, 50.0);\n\njulia> track_state = track(measurement, track_state, sampling_frequency);\n\njulia> get_carrier_doppler(track_state, 1)\n999.9999883655299 Hz\n\njulia> get_code_phase(track_state, 1)\n50.00064935064897\n\nestimate_cn0(track_state, prn) returns the C/N₀ in dB-Hz: Inf dB-Hz for a noise-free test signal, typically 30–50 dB-Hz for real ones.\n\nMultiple satellites, systems, signals and bands, antenna arrays, acquisition handoff and the accessors are covered in Tracking State; real-time loops in Track.\n\ntip: Real-time loops: hoist the correlator\nConstruct CPUThreadedDownconvertAndCorrelator (or CPUDownconvertAndCorrelator) once outside the loop and pass it via downconvert_and_correlator =. See Track.","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"index.html#Q/A","page":"Tracking.jl","text":"Why are the correlator values zero?\n\nget_last_fully_integrated_correlator returns the last completed integration, one that reached the end of its code blocks. Before the first one completes it is zero.","title":"Q/A"},{"category":"section","location":"custom_doppler_estimator.html#Custom-Doppler-Estimator","page":"Custom Doppler Estimator","text":"The Doppler estimators are TrackingLoops'. track! drives any estimator through TrackingLoops' estimator interface, so ConventionalPLLAndDLL, ConventionalAssistedPLLAndDLL, NCOReferencedPLLAndDLL, the vector loop VectorPLLAndDLL and your own (a Kalman filter, a joint-channel estimator, …) are interchangeable as doppler_estimator.","title":"Custom Doppler Estimator"},{"category":"section","location":"custom_doppler_estimator.html#Where-state-lives","page":"Custom Doppler Estimator","text":"A TrackedSat carries everything per satellite: the shared carrier/code Doppler and phase, one TrackedSignal per signal, and the estimator's per-satellite state in doppler_estimator_state. The estimator object (<: AbstractDopplerEstimator) holds configuration plus any state shared across satellites. The signal path (downconvert, correlate) is estimator-agnostic and passes doppler_estimator_state through unchanged.","title":"Where state lives"},{"category":"section","location":"custom_doppler_estimator.html#What-you-implement","page":"Custom Doppler Estimator","text":"An estimator type subtyping AbstractDopplerEstimator: configuration and shared state, no per-satellite state.\nA per-satellite state struct, e.g. SatConventionalPLLAndDLL (loop filters and seed Dopplers) or a Kalman state vector.\nAn init_estimator_state method seeding a satellite entering the track set:\nTrackingLoops.init_estimator_state(::MyEstimator, sat::TrackedSat) = MyPerSatState(...)\nIt must be pure: it is also called on the PRN-0 template sat that fixes a group's slot type, as a type probe when validating pre-built sats, and by reset_loop_filters!. Register sats into shared state in update_estimator_on_handoff, never here.\n(Optional) An update_estimator_on_handoff method, if shared state has to change when sats join (joint-channel covariance, normalization terms, …):\nTracking.update_estimator_on_handoff(est::MyEstimator, new_sats) = ...\nIt is called once per add_satellite! / merge_sats call with the dictionary of incoming sats, after per-sat seeding. The default returns est unchanged.\nThe returned estimator must keep the input's concrete type (TrackState is parameterized on it): grow shared state in place in resizable containers, or rebuild scalar fields with Setfield.@set or a copying constructor. Since TrackState is immutable, a rebuilt estimator only reaches you through the return value: write track_state = add_satellite!(track_state; ...). For in-place estimators the returned TrackState is the input itself.\nThe per-record step, in one of two ways.\nA step_loop method (TrackingLoops' per-record interface) is all track! needs. Every completed record of every signal of the satellite goes through TrackingLoops.step_loop(estimator, state, record, words, landing_sample) with the satellite's one state, the estimator-driver signal's (signals[1]) first. It returns the new state and carrier and code Dopplers; the satellite takes the driver's, as another signal's record returns the command in force. See LoopRecord for what a record carries. Tracking does the rest: the bit buffer, the C/N₀ estimators, the phase snap at sync. A TrackingLoops.reset_estimator_state(estimator, state, carrier_doppler, code_doppler) method chooses what reset_loop_filters! keeps; without one, the state is re-initialized. Such an estimator also runs unchanged in a hardware correlator's loop process.\nAn estimate_dopplers_and_filter_prompt method on TrackState{<:Any, <:MyEstimator} instead replaces the whole estimate phase, for an estimator that does not fit a per-record step (e.g. one stepping all satellites jointly). It runs once per chunk (see Chunked Doppler updates), walks track_state.groups, reads each band's BandMeasurement via get_band_id(group.band), and produces TrackedSats with new carrier_doppler/code_doppler and per-sat state.\nEach signal's records of the chunk are in its correlator_outputs::Vector{CorrelatorOutput} (zero, one or several), each with the raw correlator, its sample count, its end sample index (from the current measurement's first sample) and the replica's code phase there. Fold over them in order, write the NCO Doppler once (typically from the last), and empty correlator_outputs. The NCO Doppler is fixed across the chunk, so a discriminator needing the replica's Doppler reads the satellite's code_doppler/carrier_doppler.\ntrack! calls the mutating estimate_dopplers_and_filter_prompt!(track_state, measurements), which reassigns each group's satellites.values slots in place; define both.\nThe generic estimate phase also accepts a NamedTuple/Dict of per-band sampling frequencies (keyed by get_band_id) in place of measurements, so an external correlator producer needs no sample buffer (see External correlator producers). A custom estimator that reads only the rate should offer the same overload.","title":"What you implement"},{"category":"section","location":"custom_doppler_estimator.html#Skeleton","page":"Custom Doppler Estimator","text":"The smallest working estimator that replaces the estimate phase, with constant bodies; the structure (two structs, four methods) is what Tracking dispatches on.\n\njulia> using Tracking, TrackingLoops, GNSSSignals\n\njulia> using Tracking:\n           TrackedSat,\n           TrackState,\n           SignalGroup,\n           BandMeasurements,\n           get_band_id\n\njulia> using TrackingLoops: AbstractDopplerEstimator\n\njulia> # 1. Estimator type — config + any shared state\n       struct MyEstimator <: AbstractDopplerEstimator end\n\njulia> # 2. Per-sat state struct\n       struct SatMyEstimator end\n\njulia> # 3. Seed each sat\n       TrackingLoops.init_estimator_state(::MyEstimator, ::TrackedSat) = SatMyEstimator();\n\njulia> # 4. (Optional) shared-state update on handoff\n       Tracking.update_estimator_on_handoff(est::MyEstimator, new_sats) = est;\n\njulia> # 5a. Immutable form: a real one folds each signal's `correlator_outputs`\n       # into new Dopplers and returns a fresh `TrackState`.\n       function Tracking.estimate_dopplers_and_filter_prompt(\n           track_state::TrackState{<:Any, <:MyEstimator},\n           measurements::BandMeasurements,\n       )\n           return track_state\n       end;\n\njulia> # 5b. In-place form, what `track!` calls: writes `g.satellites.values[i]`.\n       function Tracking.estimate_dopplers_and_filter_prompt!(\n           track_state::TrackState{<:Any, <:MyEstimator},\n           measurements::BandMeasurements,\n       )\n           return track_state\n       end;\n\nPlug it into a TrackState like any other estimator:\n\njulia> using Tracking: Hz\n\njulia> track_state = TrackState(; signal = GPSL1CA(), doppler_estimator = MyEstimator());\n\njulia> track_state = add_satellite!(track_state; prn = 1, code_phase = 0.0, carrier_doppler = 1000.0Hz);\n\njulia> track_state = track(zeros(ComplexF64, 4000), track_state, 4e6Hz);\n\njulia> get_doppler_estimator_state(get_sat_state(track_state, 1))\nSatMyEstimator()\n\nThe generic estimate phase in src/conventional_pll_and_dll.jl shows the full pattern: the immutable form delegates to the in-place one, and the walk steps the estimator-driver signal (signals[1], whose records close the loops) before the passengers. That split is a convention of the per-record path only.","title":"Skeleton"},{"category":"section","location":"custom_doppler_estimator.html#What-stays-generic","page":"Custom Doppler Estimator","text":"You do not override the downconvert/correlate path; it passes doppler_estimator_state through unchanged. add_satellite! and merge_sats call init_estimator_state with the TrackState's estimator.","title":"What stays generic"},{"category":"section","location":"custom_doppler_estimator.html#API-reference","page":"Custom Doppler Estimator","text":"In the TrackingLoops manual:\n\nAbstractDopplerEstimator\nreset_estimator_state","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"custom_doppler_estimator.html#One-record-through-a-loop","page":"Custom Doppler Estimator","text":"The device-independent step lives in TrackingLoops, so the software receiver and a hardware correlator's loop process run the same arithmetic. track! passes the chunk's own replica word and no landing sample.\n\nIn the TrackingLoops manual:\n\nstep_loop\nLoopRecord\nSatConventionalPLLAndDLL","title":"One record through a loop"},{"category":"section","location":"custom_doppler_estimator.html#The-delay-aware-loop","page":"Custom Doppler Estimator","text":"NCOReferencedPLLAndDLL is for a correlator whose NCO word takes effect records after the one that motivated it, as in hardware. Given a fixed word and no landing sample, as track! supplies, it is the conventional loop to the bit at the same bandwidths; by default it pulls in at the narrow 18 Hz, as a wider loop tolerates less command delay.\n\nIn the TrackingLoops manual:\n\nNCOReferencedPLLAndDLL\nSatNCOReferencedPLLAndDLL\nNCOTimeline\nscheduled_words\nmean_nco_word\nFixedNCOWord","title":"The delay-aware loop"},{"category":"section","location":"custom_doppler_estimator.html#The-vector-loop","page":"Custom Doppler Estimator","text":"track! drives VectorPLLAndDLL(signals...) through step_loop like any other estimator. Inside, its navigation engine decodes every satellite's bits (of a plain signal, or of the data component of a pilot + data pair, e.g. VectorPLLAndDLL(GalileoE1C() => GalileoE1B()) with signals = (GalileoE1C(), GalileoE1B())), solves the PVT, and from its first fix closes all loops with a navigation filter. Read navigation_solution(estimator) and navigation_status(estimator).\n\nestimator = VectorPLLAndDLL(GPSL1CA())\ntrack_state = TrackState(; signal = GPSL1CA(), doppler_estimator = estimator)\nfor chunk in chunks\n    track!(chunk, track_state, sampling_frequency)\nend\nnavigation_solution(estimator)\n\nEach record gives it the satellite (prn), the replica's code phase at the record's end, and a time grid shared by a band's satellites: the estimate phase adds the band's running offset (TrackState's sample_offsets, advanced by every track!). Step every satellite at least every half vt_update_interval (50 ms by default), which any track! on a shorter chunk does.\n\nThe engine and the offsets belong together: a TrackState built afresh around an estimator that has run starts every band at 0, so pass it the old state's sample_offsets (see TrackState). To branch a state, deepcopy it; track shares the estimator with its input.\n\nIn the vector loop the navigation filter alone sets a satellite's carrier NCO; a residual PLL follows the carrier on the prompts without steering it, and carrier_loop_stage reports VECTOR_LOOP. reset_loop_filters! re-seeds the inner loop and keeps the satellite in the vector loop: it drops the code correction (the converged Dopplers contain it) until the filter's next cycle, and keeps the carrier command (they do not).\n\nIn the TrackingLoops manual:\n\nVectorPLLAndDLL\nSatVectorPLLAndDLL\nLoopRecord","title":"The vector loop"},{"category":"function","location":"custom_doppler_estimator.html#TrackingLoops.init_estimator_state","page":"Custom Doppler Estimator","text":"init_estimator_state(estimator, sat::TrackedSat)\n\nBuild the per-satellite Doppler-estimator state for sat: TrackingLoops' init_estimator_state on the satellite's estimator-driver signal (signals[1]) and its handover Dopplers. A custom estimator defines either this form or TrackingLoops' four-argument one.\n\nIt must be pure: it also builds the PRN-0 template sat that fixes a group's slot type, probes types when validating pre-built sats, and re-seeds in reset_loop_filters!. Register satellites in shared state in update_estimator_on_handoff instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TrackingLoops.init_estimator_state"},{"category":"function","location":"custom_doppler_estimator.html#Tracking.update_estimator_on_handoff","page":"Custom Doppler Estimator","text":"update_estimator_on_handoff(estimator, _new_sats)\n\n\nUpdate an estimator's cross-satellite shared state when satellites enter the track set. Called once per handoff (merge_sats, add_satellite, add_satellite!) with the incoming satellites, after init_estimator_state. The default returns estimator unchanged.\n\nThe result must have the input's concrete type (TrackState is parameterized on it): grow resizable containers in place, or rebuild with Setfield.@set or a copying constructor. The returned TrackState carries the result, so callers of the in-place add_satellite! too must keep using the returned state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.update_estimator_on_handoff"},{"category":"function","location":"custom_doppler_estimator.html#Tracking.estimate_dopplers_and_filter_prompt","page":"Custom Doppler Estimator","text":"estimate_dopplers_and_filter_prompt(\n    track_state,\n    sampling_frequencies\n)\n\n\nFold every completed correlator record of every satellite through the estimator's TrackingLoops step_loop (any estimator implementing it works), giving the Dopplers for the next replicas and the post-correlation-filtered prompts.\n\nThe second argument is a BandMeasurements NamedTuple (the track path) or a NamedTuple/Dict mapping each get_band_id to its sampling frequency: the entry point for an external correlator producer (e.g. an FPGA), which needs no sample buffer. See External correlator producers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.estimate_dopplers_and_filter_prompt"},{"category":"function","location":"custom_doppler_estimator.html#Tracking.estimate_dopplers_and_filter_prompt!","page":"Custom Doppler Estimator","text":"estimate_dopplers_and_filter_prompt!(\n    track_state,\n    sampling_frequencies\n)\n\n\nIn-place version of estimate_dopplers_and_filter_prompt, taking the same second argument: overwrites each group's TrackedSat slots and returns track_state, allocation-free in steady state under track!'s preconditions.\n\nIt reads only the per-band sampling frequency (turning integrated_samples into an integration time), and consumes and clears each signal's correlator_outputs, whether from the software correlate phase or from append_correlator_output!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.estimate_dopplers_and_filter_prompt!"},{"category":"section","location":"loop_filter.html#Loop-Filter","page":"Loop Filter","text":"The loop filters are provided by TrackingLoopFilters.jl. This includes:\n\nfirst order loop filter FirstOrderLF\nsecond order bilinear loop filter SecondOrderBilinearLF\nsecond order boxcar loop filter SecondOrderBoxcarLF\nthird order bilinear loop filter ThirdOrderBilinearLF\nthird order boxcar loop filter ThirdOrderBoxcarLF\nthird order assisted bilinear loop filter ThirdOrderAssistedBilinearLF (combines PLL and FLL)","title":"Loop Filter"},{"category":"section","location":"loop_filter.html#Default-Configuration","page":"Loop Filter","text":"The default Doppler estimator is ConventionalAssistedPLLAndDLL which uses:\n\nThirdOrderAssistedBilinearLF for the carrier loop (FLL-assisted PLL for improved dynamics)\nSecondOrderBilinearLF for the code loop\n\nWhen TrackState builds the default estimator from a signal-tuple declaration, the carrier loop is staged (see CarrierLoopStage): it pulls in as an FLL-assisted PLL at a wide 50 Hz with a 5 Hz FLL-assist bandwidth, drops the FLL once a phase-lock indicator reads lock, and later narrows to a narrow 18 Hz. The code bandwidth is a flat 1 Hz. All are one-sided noise bandwidths BL (Kaplan & Hegarty), for every signal. The stages only advance, and reset_loop_filters! keeps the stage reached; a satellite added anew starts over. Read a satellite's stage with carrier_loop_stage(get_doppler_estimator_state(sat)).\n\nWhere the replica wipes every sign modulation off the prompt (a dataless signal synced to its secondary code) the carrier discriminators turn four-quadrant.\n\nThe defaults are not scaled with the integration time; each is capped at filter time by its stability product BL · T_int: the PLL by 0.09, the FLL path by 0.02, the code loop by 0.018 (effective_carrier_loop_filter_bandwidth and siblings). The bandwidths that end up in the loop are:\n\nIntegration time Wide BL Narrow BL FLL-assist BL Code BL\n1 ms (L1 C/A, L5I, …) 50 Hz 18 Hz 5 Hz 1 Hz\n4 ms (E1B / E1C) 22.5 Hz 18 Hz 5 Hz 1 Hz\n10 ms (L1C-D/P, B1C) 9 Hz 9 Hz 2 Hz 1 Hz\n20 ms (L1 C/A or L2 CM after sync) 4.5 Hz 4.5 Hz 1 Hz 0.9 Hz\n1.5 s (L2 CL) 0.06 Hz 0.06 Hz 0.013 Hz 0.012 Hz\n\nThe TrackingLoops manual (\"Loop-filter bandwidths\") gives the rationale for the bandwidths and caps.\n\nBefore TrackingLoops 4 the carrier filter was fed radians but its output read as Hz, making the loop about 5.6× wider than configured (≈ 100 Hz for 18 Hz); to get approximately that loop back, set both wide_carrier_loop_filter_bandwidth and narrow_carrier_loop_filter_bandwidth to 85.0Hz. Before TrackingLoops 5 the loop ran FLL-assisted at one carrier bandwidth throughout; pass equal wide and narrow bandwidths for one PLL bandwidth.\n\nOverride per signal by defining methods of default_wide_carrier_loop_filter_bandwidth, default_narrow_carrier_loop_filter_bandwidth, default_fll_assist_loop_filter_bandwidth / default_code_loop_filter_bandwidth, or override at construction time by passing your own doppler_estimator = to TrackState.","title":"Default Configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"loop_filter.html#Doppler-Estimators","page":"Loop Filter","text":"In the TrackingLoops manual:\n\nConventionalPLLAndDLL\nConventionalAssistedPLLAndDLL\ndefault_wide_carrier_loop_filter_bandwidth\ndefault_narrow_carrier_loop_filter_bandwidth\ndefault_fll_assist_loop_filter_bandwidth\ndefault_code_loop_filter_bandwidth\neffective_carrier_loop_filter_bandwidth\neffective_fll_assist_loop_filter_bandwidth\neffective_code_loop_filter_bandwidth\nLoopBandwidths\nCarrierLoopStage\ncarrier_loop_stage\nphase_lock_indicator","title":"Doppler Estimators"},{"category":"section","location":"loop_filter.html#Resetting-loop-filters","page":"Loop Filter","text":"After changing a signal's integration length with set_preferred_num_code_blocks_to_integrate!, reset the affected loop filters so the old integration length's filter state does not leak into the new one.","title":"Resetting loop filters"},{"category":"section","location":"loop_filter.html#Custom-Configuration","page":"Loop Filter","text":"You can customize the loop filters and bandwidths when creating the Doppler estimator:\n\njulia> using Tracking, TrackingLoops, GNSSSignals, TrackingLoopFilters\n\njulia> using Tracking: Hz\n\njulia> # Use non-assisted PLL with custom loop filter types\n       doppler_estimator = ConventionalPLLAndDLL(\n           ThirdOrderBilinearLF,      # carrier loop filter type\n           SecondOrderBilinearLF;     # code loop filter type\n           wide_carrier_loop_filter_bandwidth = 30.0Hz,\n           narrow_carrier_loop_filter_bandwidth = 15.0Hz,\n           code_loop_filter_bandwidth = 0.5Hz\n       );\n\njulia> track_state = TrackState(; signal = GPSL1CA(), doppler_estimator);\n\njulia> track_state = add_satellite!(track_state; prn = 1, code_phase = 50.0, carrier_doppler = 1000.0Hz);","title":"Custom Configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"loop_filter.html#Custom-Loop-Filters","page":"Loop Filter","text":"You can implement a custom loop filter MyLoopFilter <: AbstractLoopFilter. In this case, a specialized filter_loop function is needed. For more information refer to TrackingLoopFilters.jl.","title":"Custom Loop Filters"},{"category":"section","location":"loop_filter.html#Custom-Doppler-Estimator","page":"Loop Filter","text":"To replace the loop-filter-based estimator with a different algorithm (Kalman filter, joint-channel estimator, …), see the dedicated guide in custom_doppler_estimator.md.","title":"Custom Doppler Estimator"},{"category":"function","location":"loop_filter.html#Tracking.reset_loop_filters!","page":"Loop Filter","text":"reset_loop_filters!(track_state)\n\n\nRe-seed the Doppler-estimator state of every satellite (or one addressed satellite) from its current Doppler. For the conventional PLL/DLL this zeroes the loop-filter integrators and keeps carrier_doppler, code_doppler, any per-satellite bandwidth override and the carrier-loop stage. Each signal's last_fully_integrated_filtered_prompt is cleared, so the first FLL update does not measure a rotation spanning the old integration interval.\n\nUse it when a signal's integration length changes mid-track, e.g. GPS L5I from 1 ms to 10 ms via set_preferred_num_code_blocks_to_integrate!: the integrator state does not carry over to the new update interval, and the transient can drag the loop out of lock.\n\nNo satellite id resets every satellite; (group, prn) or (single-group) prn resets one. Mutates track_state and returns it. Works for any AbstractDopplerEstimator through its init_estimator_state hook.\n\nset_preferred_num_code_blocks_to_integrate!(track_state, 1, GPSL5I, 10)\nreset_loop_filters!(track_state, 1)          # clean handoff for PRN 1\nreset_loop_filters!(track_state)             # …or reset every satellite\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.reset_loop_filters!"},{"category":"section","location":"cn0_estimator.html#CN0-Estimator","page":"CN0 Estimator","text":"The CN0 (carrier-to-noise density ratio) estimator measures signal quality in dB-Hz. Each TrackedSignal on a TrackedSat holds its own estimator, so a multi-signal satellite reports one CN0 per signal.\n\nnote: Every signal of a satellite is estimated, not just the driver\nEach signal's estimator is fed its own prompt once per completed record; the estimator-driver signal has no special role, and the values differ: on GPS L1C the pilot carries ~75 % of the power and the data component ~25 %, 4.8 dB apart.Nothing inside Tracking reads estimate_cn0; the per-record update costs well under a per cent of the record's correlation work (~40 ns against ~9 µs for a two-signal satellite over 4000 samples). Switch a signal off with NoCN0Estimator for clarity, not speed.","title":"CN0 Estimator"},{"category":"section","location":"cn0_estimator.html#Choosing-an-estimator","page":"CN0 Estimator","text":" NoiseRefCN0Estimator (default) NWPRCN0Estimator MomentsCN0Estimator NoCN0Estimator\nNeeds the band's samples (or a noise observation)? yes no no no\nNeeds bit sync / a coherent window? no yes no no\nNeeds to know the window length M? no yes no no\nWorks on GPS L1C-D, Galileo E1B, pre-sync secondary codes? yes no — falls back yes, at its floor n/a\nBias, thermal only <0.03 dB ≈+0.05 dB everywhere ≈27.6 dB-Hz floor on noise n/a\nHigh-C/N₀ bias floor ≈0.13 dB at 45, ≈0.40 at 50 (self-leakage) none none n/a\nHigh-C/N₀ σ keeps improving, until the row above dominates saturates at √(M/((M−1)K)) ≈0.5 dB — n/a\nDegenerate outputs none -Inf/Inf outside 1 < μ̂ < M — 5.6 % of estimates at 20 dB-Hz none always -Inf\nPhase-noise / residual-Doppler sensitivity immune (non-coherent) sensitive — the coherent sum loses power; −12 dB at 120 Hz of handoff Doppler, see Residual Doppler at acquisition handoff immune n/a\nCost per-record arithmetic + one despread per signal per chunk per-record arithmetic per-record arithmetic none\n\nThe \"degenerate\" row counts unusable outputs: estimate_cn0 pools every buffered window and range-checks the pooled ratio once.","title":"Choosing an estimator"},{"category":"section","location":"cn0_estimator.html#Which-one-do-I-use?","page":"CN0 Estimator","text":"Sample-driven track! → NoiseRefCN0Estimator, the default: the signal gets a CorrelatorNoiseEstimator that track! fills.\nCorrelator outputs from hardware, with a noise observation → the same, plus one append_noise_observation! per signal per fold (see Noise Estimator).\nCorrelator outputs from hardware, without one → NWPRCN0Estimator(signal), accepting its fallback on the signals it cannot serve.\nA signal whose C/N₀ nobody reads → NoCN0Estimator; see Not measuring a signal at all.\nMomentsCN0Estimator is a fallback component and prompt-stream tool, not a standalone default; see Why not the moment method.","title":"Which one do I use?"},{"category":"section","location":"cn0_estimator.html#The-default:-C/N-against-a-measured-noise-floor","page":"CN0 Estimator","text":"NoiseRefCN0Estimator computes, per record,\n\nĈ/N₀ = ⟨|P|²⟩ / N̂₀ − 1/T\n\nwith N̂₀ the measured noise density (see Noise Estimator) and T the record's integration time, and averages the per-record terms. It is non-coherent: no bit sync, no window, no fallback, the same behaviour on every signal.\n\nIn the TrackingLoops manual:\n\nNoiseRefCN0Estimator\ndefault_cn0_estimator","title":"The default: C/N₀ against a measured noise floor"},{"category":"section","location":"cn0_estimator.html#Getting-the-low-C/N-variance","page":"CN0 Estimator","text":"Below about 28 dB-Hz a non-coherent estimator is the noisier one at matched record length (squaring loss: NWPR sums M prompts coherently first, better by √(M−1)). But a coherent sum of five 1 ms prompts is one 5 ms prompt, so take that integration in the correlator instead, with set_preferred_num_code_blocks_to_integrate!: the noise reference then beats NWPR at every C/N₀. Modelled over 100 ms, 4000 trials × 9 seeds, relative σ in dB:\n\nC/N₀ NWPR (M = 5) NoiseRef, 1 ms records NoiseRef, 5 ms records\n20 2.96 4.79 2.76\n25 1.50 1.72 1.24\n30 0.90 0.75 0.65\n40 0.54 0.20 0.20\n50 0.50 0.06 0.06\n\nThis is a trade: a longer record also narrows the loop (the carrier bandwidth is capped at 0.09 / T_int), and before bit/secondary sync records are one block whatever you configure.","title":"Getting the low-C/N₀ variance"},{"category":"section","location":"cn0_estimator.html#The-one-bias-it-carries","page":"CN0 Estimator","text":"Despreading with a wrong PRN also collects the tracked satellite's own power, ε_self/N₀ = C/f_chip, so on GPS L1 C/A C/N₀ reads low by 0.013 dB at 35 dB-Hz, 0.042 at 40, 0.13 at 45 and 0.40 at 50; ten times less at 10.23 Mcps (GPS L5, Galileo E5a). It is deliberately not corrected, as that would couple every satellite's C/N₀ to the others' (see NoiseRefCN0Estimator).","title":"The one bias it carries"},{"category":"section","location":"cn0_estimator.html#NWPRCN0Estimator:-the-coherent-window-estimator","page":"CN0 Estimator","text":"Van Dierendonck's narrowband/wideband power ratio: per window of M records the coherent power NBP = |Σ prompt|² against the incoherent WBP = Σ |prompt|², summed over the windows in num_prompts_for_cn0_estimation records. It is the estimator for a correlator-ingest path that cannot report a noise observation.\n\nIn the TrackingLoops manual:\n\nNWPRCN0Estimator\nNWPRCN0Estimator(::AbstractGNSSSignal)","title":"NWPRCN0Estimator: the coherent-window estimator"},{"category":"section","location":"cn0_estimator.html#Why-not-the-moment-method","page":"CN0 Estimator","text":"The Moments Method (M2M4, MomentsCN0Estimator) is a moment ratio, and at a finite window its sample moments manufacture signal power out of noise. On synthetic prompts (amplitude √(C/N₀·T) in unit-variance complex noise), 100 one-millisecond records, 400 realizations:\n\ntrue C/N₀ M2M4 median p10 p90 NWPR (M = 20) median\nnoise only 27.6 22.6 31.1 −Inf (\"no signal\")\n20 dB-Hz 27.7 22.5 30.9 19.9\n25 dB-Hz 27.9 23.4 30.9 25.0\n30 dB-Hz 30.5 27.3 32.6 30.0\n35 dB-Hz 35.3 33.9 36.4 35.0\n45 dB-Hz 45.1 44.3 45.9 45.0\n\nM2M4 is blind below ~30 dB-Hz: pure noise clears a 30 dB-Hz threshold 19 % of the time, 28 dB-Hz 45 % and 25 dB-Hz 76 %, so the usual code-lock hysteresis (lock at 34, drop at 25) cannot be built on it. NWPR at M = 20 has 0 % false alarms from 20 to 32 dB-Hz (Falletti, Pini & Lo Presti, IEEE T-AES 47(1):420–437, 2011; issue #217).\n\nEnd to end through track on a data-modulated GPS L1 C/A signal at the (shorter) default windows, 600 ms per run, medians over 16 seeds:\n\ntrue C/N₀ NWPR (default) p10 p90 M2M4\nnoise only −Inf in 12 of 16 runs   27.9\n20 dB-Hz 18.3 6.0 22.2 27.6\n25 dB-Hz 23.9 16.3 26.3 28.3\n30 dB-Hz 29.0 27.4 30.3 30.8\n35 dB-Hz 34.1 33.3 35.0 35.3\n45 dB-Hz 44.3 43.7 45.2 45.4\n\nOn pure noise pre-sync, where a code-lock detector decides, the default clears a 20 dB-Hz threshold in 4 % of updates and 25 dB-Hz in 0 % (200 runs, highest value 22.4); M2M4 on the same runs clears 25 dB-Hz in 67 %. The price is the coherent sum's sensitivity to anything that decorrelates the prompts (see What the estimator returns).","title":"Why not the moment method"},{"category":"section","location":"cn0_estimator.html#The-window-follows-the-navigation-bits","page":"CN0 Estimator","text":"A window must not straddle a bit flip (~7 dB), so each prompt comes with the navigation-bit state in a CN0UpdateContext and the window follows it; the cases are tabulated under NWPRCN0Estimator. Before sync, a data-bearing signal without a secondary code uses an unaligned num_presync_narrowband_code_blocks window (5), since bit sync takes seconds and fails below ~30 dB-Hz; it costs ~0.6 dB below 30 dB-Hz on GPS L1 C/A. Where no window is admissible (a secondary code before sync, one symbol per code block, or a record as long as its window, e.g. with set_preferred_num_code_blocks_to_integrate! at a whole bit) the MomentsCN0Estimator fallback, and its floor, is reported.\n\nwarning: The moment ratio's noise floor is there on data-only signals\nGPS L1C-D and Galileo E1B carry one navigation symbol per code block, so the longest coherent window is one record, where NBP == WBP. They keep the moment ratio for good: pure noise reads ~27.6 dB-Hz on L1C-D and E1B, as does any signal with a secondary code (GPS L5, L2C, Galileo E1C, E5a) before its sync.No ~100-record window fixes this: without coherence only the prompt magnitudes carry information. (A phase-blind (E|z|)²/E|z|² estimator cuts false alarms, 42 % against 76 % at 25 dB-Hz on pure noise, but its median on noise is still ~27.5 dB-Hz.)The default NoiseRefCN0Estimator has no window, so these are ordinary records to it (issue #217). On NWPR, take the lock decision from the pilot of the pair (L1C-D with L1C-P, E1B with E1C), give the data component a NoCN0Estimator, or use NWPRCN0Estimator(signal; fallback = NoCN0Estimator()), which reports -Inf dB-Hz exactly when no coherent window is available.","title":"The window follows the navigation bits"},{"category":"section","location":"cn0_estimator.html#The-window-is-capped-by-the-loop's-coherence-time","page":"CN0 Estimator","text":"The windows tile the bit from its start but stay shorter than a bit (num_narrowband_code_blocks, ~5 ms): a longer window buys little, while decoherence over it costs a bias averaging cannot remove. The default staged loop has narrowed to 18 Hz by the time a locked satellite fades (see Loop Filter) and holds phase over a whole bit, so for it the window length hardly matters; the short default protects a loop that has not narrowed or runs wider. Raise num_narrowband_code_blocks for a pilot, a narrow carrier loop or a signal that is never weak. Measurements are under NWPRCN0Estimator.","title":"The window is capped by the loop's coherence time"},{"category":"section","location":"cn0_estimator.html#Configuring-the-estimator","page":"CN0 Estimator","text":"num_prompts_for_cn0_estimation (default 100, ~100 ms for L1 C/A) sets how many records the estimate averages over (also NWPR's fallback):\n\njulia> using Tracking, GNSSSignals\n\njulia> using Tracking: Hz\n\njulia> track_state = TrackState(; signal = GPSL1CA());\n\njulia> sat = TrackedSat(GPSL1CA(), 1, 50.0, 1000.0Hz; num_prompts_for_cn0_estimation = 200);\n\njulia> track_state = add_satellite!(track_state, :default, sat);\n\njulia> get_prn(track_state, 1)\n1\n\nTo choose a different estimator, or configure NWPR's windows, pass a cn0_estimator; any AbstractCN0Estimator is stored as is (it is a type parameter of TrackedSignal):\n\njulia> using Tracking, TrackingLoops, GNSSSignals\n\njulia> using Tracking: Hz\n\njulia> sat = TrackedSat(GPSL1CA(), 1, 50.0, 1000.0Hz; cn0_estimator = MomentsCN0Estimator(100));\n\njulia> Tracking.get_cn0_estimator(sat) isa MomentsCN0Estimator\ntrue\n\njulia> sat = TrackedSat(\n           GPSL1CA(),\n           1,\n           50.0,\n           1000.0Hz;\n           cn0_estimator = NWPRCN0Estimator(\n               GPSL1CA();\n               num_records = 400,                       # ~400 ms of memory\n               num_presync_narrowband_code_blocks = 0,  # M2M4 until bit sync\n           ),\n       );\n\njulia> Tracking.get_cn0_estimator(sat).num_records\n400\n\nPassing the signal to NWPRCN0Estimator sizes its window for the code period: 5 blocks for a 1 ms code, 2 for GPS L1C-P's 10 ms one (the bare constructor's 5 would be 50 ms there).\n\nEach signal needs its own estimator instance (they buffer into a vector, so a shared one corrupts both); the multi-signal constructor takes one per signal:\n\nTrackedSat(\n    (GPSL1C_P(), GPSL1CA()),\n    prn,\n    code_phase,\n    carrier_doppler;\n    cn0_estimator = (NoiseRefCN0Estimator(), MomentsCN0Estimator(100)),\n)\n\nOnly a signal whose estimator reads a density is provisioned a noise source, so the case above runs one despread per chunk, for the noise-referenced signal; a signal on NWPR runs none. The floor is per signal, not per band (see Why per signal and not per RF band), so the cost is one despread per noise-referenced signal per chunk, against n_sats tracking correlations apiece.","title":"Configuring the estimator"},{"category":"section","location":"cn0_estimator.html#Not-measuring-a-signal-at-all","page":"CN0 Estimator","text":"NoCN0Estimator keeps no state, does no per-record work and reports -Inf dB-Hz (not NaN, which compares >= true against every threshold). Two uses:\n\n# the non-driver signal of a pair whose C/N₀ nobody reads\nTrackedSat(\n    (GPSL1C_P(), GPSL1C_D()),\n    prn,\n    code_phase,\n    carrier_doppler;\n    cn0_estimator = (NoiseRefCN0Estimator(), NoCN0Estimator()),\n)\n\n# NWPR with no moment-ratio floor underneath it: \"no estimate\" until a coherent\n# window exists, instead of the moment ratio's ~27.6 dB-Hz on noise\nNWPRCN0Estimator(GPSL1C_D(); fallback = NoCN0Estimator())\n\nThe second only matters on NWPR (the default has no fallback): it turns the fallback's noise floor into -Inf wherever no coherent window exists (see the warning above).\n\nIn the TrackingLoops manual:\n\nMomentsCN0Estimator\nNoCN0Estimator","title":"Not measuring a signal at all"},{"category":"section","location":"cn0_estimator.html#What-the-estimator-returns","page":"CN0 Estimator","text":"Before the first record (e.g. right after add_satellite!): -Inf dB-Hz, so a lock detector cannot clear on it.\nA noise-free signal: nothing to divide by, so a very large value; NWPR and the moment estimator report Inf dB-Hz (μ̂ = M). The Quick start shows this.\nA noisy signal: converges to the true CN0 as the buffers fill; real signals typically land in 30–50 dB-Hz.\nNoise only: the default reports -Inf dB-Hz once the averaged terms fail to clear zero, which is most of the time, and has no positive floor; NWPR reports -Inf when μ̂ ≤ 1. That is the median, not a guarantee: code that averages the estimate must handle the infinities.\nWhile the loops struggle: NWPR measures coherent C/N₀, so phase noise counts against it: at 1 ms records and default windows ~0.9 dB low at a true 35 dB-Hz, ~1.1 dB at 25 and ~1.7 dB at 20, much worse with num_narrowband_code_blocks past the loop's coherence time. The default is immune: through track! at a true 45 dB-Hz it reads 44.9 at 1-block and 44.7 at 20-block records, where NWPR falls to 32.9.\n\nAll estimators report C/N₀ against the effective noise density: the other satellites' cross-correlation power ε is seen by NWPR and the noise reference alike, so both report C/(N₀+ε), as other receivers do. Only the reference's self-leakage differs (see The one bias it carries).\n\nnote: An antenna array's floor follows each satellite's own beamformer\nThe prompt is post-AbstractPostCorrFilter, so the floor must go through the same weights. With num_ants > 1 the noise reference's window (one per signal) holds the M×M spatial noise covariance R̂, and each satellite reduces it at C/N₀ time through its own current weights,N̂₀ = wᴴ R̂ wexact for any fixed w since E[|wᴴn|²] = wᴴRw. Hence get_weights is required of a post-correlation filter, and the contract is linear in the antennas. DefaultPostCorrFilter selects the last antenna, whose diagonal entry wᴴR̂w then is, as in a single-antenna run on that column.","title":"What the estimator returns"},{"category":"section","location":"cn0_estimator.html#Residual-Doppler-at-acquisition-handoff","page":"CN0 Estimator","text":"Acquisition hands over a Doppler good to a search bin (commonly ±100 Hz), which the loop takes tens of milliseconds to pull in, just when a lock detector decides whether to keep the satellite. Through track! at a true 40 dB-Hz, 1 ms records, seeded with a carrier-Doppler error, median of 5 seeds over the first 60 ms:\n\nΔf real within-record loss NWPR Moments NoiseRef\n0 Hz 0.00 dB 39.8 40.5 40.1\n30 Hz −0.01 dB 39.8 40.4 40.1\n60 Hz −0.05 dB 39.2 40.4 40.1\n120 Hz −0.21 dB 27.6 40.5 39.9\n250 Hz −0.91 dB −Inf 40.1 39.1\n\nAt 120 Hz NWPR under-reports by 12 dB, and at 250 Hz reports -Inf dB-Hz on a satellite being tracked perfectly well. Across the window a residual Δf turns the prompt by Δf·M·T cycles: 0.6 at 120 Hz over the default 5 ms, spreading the five phasors over 216° and costing NBP ~5.9 dB; μ̂ collapses toward 1, and past 1.25 cycles μ̂ ≤ 1 gives -Inf. This is the constraint M·T ≪ 1/(2·Δf) under NWPRCN0Estimator, which at the default window means Δf ≪ 100 Hz.\n\nThe non-coherent estimators see only the real within-record loss, sinc²(Δf·T): 0.21 dB at 120 Hz, 0.91 dB at 250 Hz (40 − 0.91 ≈ the measured 39.1). The open-loop noise reference cannot be perturbed by any satellite's Doppler error.\n\nAfter 300 ms NWPR recovers to 39.2 dB-Hz at every offset up to 120 Hz. At 250 Hz it stays at -Inf and the noise reference reads 38.9: the loop never pulled in, so the residual and its 0.9 dB loss remain.\n\nOn NWPR, a shorter pre-sync window helps, though a two-record window is also the noisiest:\n\nnum_presync_narrowband_code_blocks Δf·M·T at 120 Hz reported\n5 (default) 0.60 cycles 27.6\n3 0.36 cycles 32.4\n2 0.24 cycles 34.9\n\nNWPRCN0Estimator(GPSL1CA(); num_presync_narrowband_code_blocks = 2)\n\nA noisy L1 C/A signal at a known 45 dB-Hz, through 25 1-ms tracking cycles:\n\njulia> using Tracking, GNSSSignals, Random\n\njulia> using Tracking: Hz\n\njulia> using GNSSSignals: gen_code, get_code_frequency\n\njulia> function run_cn0_demo()\n           sys = GPSL1CA()\n           fs = 4e6Hz\n           num_samples = 4000  # 1 ms at 4 MHz\n           prn = 1\n           cn0_db_hz = 45.0\n           sigma = sqrt((fs/Hz) / 10^(cn0_db_hz/10) / 2)\n           code_freq = get_code_frequency(sys)\n           rng = MersenneTwister(0)\n           track_state = TrackState(; signal = GPSL1CA())\n           track_state =\n               add_satellite!(track_state; prn, code_phase = 0.0, carrier_doppler = 0.0Hz)\n           for _ = 1:25\n               clean = gen_code(num_samples, sys, prn, fs, code_freq, 0.0)\n               noise = sigma .* randn(rng, ComplexF64, num_samples)\n               track_state = track(clean .+ noise, track_state, fs)\n           end\n           estimate_cn0(track_state, 1)\n       end;\n\njulia> run_cn0_demo()\n47.9 dB-Hz\n\nWith unit-amplitude code samples, C/N₀ = 1 / (σ² / Fs) gives σ² = Fs / 10^(CN0_dB/10), split across real and imaginary parts. 25 records fill a quarter of the default 100-record window, so a few dB of spread is expected.\n\nIn the TrackingLoops manual:\n\nestimate_cn0","title":"Residual Doppler at acquisition handoff"},{"category":"section","location":"cn0_estimator.html#Custom-CN0-Estimators","page":"CN0 Estimator","text":"In the TrackingLoops manual:\n\nAbstractCN0Estimator\nCN0UpdateContext\n\nSubtype AbstractCN0Estimator and implement:\n\nTrackingLoops.update(cn0_estimator::MyCN0Estimator, prompt), returning a new estimator with the prompt added;\nestimate_cn0(cn0_estimator::MyCN0Estimator, integration_time), returning dB-Hz; integration_time is the last record's, i.e. get_last_fully_integrated_integration_time.\n\nTo use the navigation-bit grid (boundaries, sync, soft bits), implement TrackingLoops.update(cn0_estimator::MyCN0Estimator, prompt, context::CN0UpdateContext) instead; the tracking loop calls this form, whose default drops the context.\n\nIn the TrackingLoops manual:\n\nupdate(::MomentsCN0Estimator, ::Any)\nupdate(::AbstractCN0Estimator, ::Any, ::CN0UpdateContext)\nupdate(::NWPRCN0Estimator, ::Any, ::CN0UpdateContext)\nupdate(::NWPRCN0Estimator, ::Any)\nupdate(::NoiseRefCN0Estimator, ::Any, ::CN0UpdateContext)\nupdate(::NoiseRefCN0Estimator, ::Any)\nupdate(::NoCN0Estimator, ::Any)\n\nPlug it in with the cn0_estimator keyword of TrackedSignal or TrackedSat:\n\njulia> using Tracking, TrackingLoops, GNSSSignals\n\njulia> using Tracking: Hz\n\njulia> struct MyCN0Estimator <: TrackingLoops.AbstractCN0Estimator end\n\njulia> TrackingLoops.update(estimator::MyCN0Estimator, prompt) = estimator;\n\njulia> TrackingLoops.estimate_cn0(::MyCN0Estimator, integration_time) = 42.0 * Tracking.dBHz;\n\njulia> sat = TrackedSat(GPSL1CA(), 1, 50.0, 1000.0Hz; cn0_estimator = MyCN0Estimator());\n\njulia> estimate_cn0(sat)\n42.0 dB-Hz","title":"Custom CN0 Estimators"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking-State","page":"Tracking State","text":"The tracking state nests as TrackState → SignalGroup → TrackedSat → TrackedSignal:\n\nTrackState: a NamedTuple of SignalGroups and the Doppler estimator.\nSignalGroup: satellites with the same signal tuple, and so one concrete TrackedSat type (the hot loop's type stability), on one RF band and antenna array.\nTrackedSat: the carrier/code Doppler and phase its signals share, a tuple of TrackedSignals, and the Doppler-estimator state.\nTrackedSignal: correlator, post-correlation filter, C/N₀ estimator, bit buffer and integration progress.","title":"Tracking State"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#Estimator-driver-signal","page":"Tracking State","text":"The first signal of each group's tuple is the estimator-driver signal, the one the Doppler estimator updates the shared carrier and code Doppler from. The conventional estimators (ConventionalPLLAndDLL, ConventionalAssistedPLLAndDLL) run their discriminators on it and take its default loop bandwidths (a carrier loop pulling in at 50 Hz and narrowing to 18 Hz, 1 Hz code). A custom AbstractDopplerEstimator may use any signal.\n\nBit sync, the post-correlation filter and the C/N₀ estimator run per signal, so a multi-signal satellite has one C/N₀ per signal; see CN0 Estimator and NoCN0Estimator, the per-signal opt-out.","title":"Estimator-driver signal"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#Choosing-a-TrackState-constructor","page":"Tracking State","text":"The right constructor depends on when you know which satellites you'll track.","title":"Choosing a TrackState constructor"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#One-shot-scripts:-acquire-once,-then-track","page":"Tracking State","text":"The Acquisition extension's constructors derive signals, loop bandwidths and satellites from AcquisitionResults:\n\nusing Acquisition  # loads the extension; required for TrackState(acq...)\n\n# Single satellite\nacq = acquire(GPSL1CA(), data, sampling_frequency, 7)\ntrack_state = TrackState(acq)\n\n# Many satellites, one signal\nacqs = acquire(GPSL1CA(), data, sampling_frequency, 1:32)\ntrack_state = TrackState(filter(is_detected, acqs))\n\n# Many satellites, multiple signals\nacqs = vcat(\n    acquire(GPSL1CA(),    data, sampling_frequency, 1:32),\n    acquire(GalileoE1B(), data, sampling_frequency, 1:36),\n)\ntrack_state = TrackState(filter(is_detected, acqs);\n    signals = (gps = (GPSL1CA(),), gal = (GalileoE1B(),)),\n)\n\nadd_satellite! can later add satellites only for the signals those acqs declared, as the groups are frozen at construction. To track more signals, build empty and populate instead.","title":"One-shot scripts: acquire once, then track"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#Real-time-/-repeating-loops:-build-empty,-then-populate","page":"Tracking State","text":"A receiver that re-acquires periodically builds an empty TrackState once with TrackState(; signal = ...) or TrackState(; signals = (...)) and adds and removes satellites with add_satellite! and remove_satellite!:\n\n# Build once\ntrack_state = TrackState(;\n    signals = (gps = (GPSL1CA(),), gal = (GalileoE1B(),)),\n)\n\n# In your acquisition loop\nwhile running\n    acqs = acquire(GPSL1CA(), latest_chunk, sampling_frequency, candidate_prns)\n    track_state = add_satellite!(track_state, filter(is_detected, acqs))   # routes each acq to the matching group\n    track_state = track(latest_chunk, track_state, sampling_frequency)\nend\n\nThe TrackState's concrete type stays fixed as satellites come and go. signal = GPSL1CA() is short for signals = (default = (GPSL1CA(),),); with one group, add_satellite! may omit group =.","title":"Real-time / repeating loops: build empty, then populate"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#Power-user:-pre-built-TrackedSats","page":"Tracking State","text":"To change the correlator or post-correlation filter type, build the TrackedSats yourself, pass them to TrackState(signal, sats), and add more with add_satellite!(track_state, group, sat). The single-signal constructor:\n\nTrackedSat(\n    signal,                # e.g. GPSL1CA()\n    prn::Int,\n    code_phase,\n    carrier_doppler;\n    # all kwargs below are optional and have signal-derived defaults\n    doppler_estimator     = ConventionalAssistedPLLAndDLL(...),\n    num_ants              = NumAnts(1),\n    correlator            = get_default_correlator(signal, num_ants),\n    carrier_phase         = 0.0,\n    code_doppler          = carrier_doppler * get_code_center_frequency_ratio(signal),\n    num_prompts_for_cn0_estimation = 100,\n    cn0_estimator         = TrackingLoops.default_cn0_estimator(signal, num_prompts_for_cn0_estimation),\n    post_corr_filter      = DefaultPostCorrFilter(),\n)\n\ncn0_estimator takes any AbstractCN0Estimator; as a type parameter of TrackedSignal, unlike the correlator and filter, it may change the satellite's type (see CN0 Estimator).\n\nAn example with a narrower correlator, a trivial mean-of-antennas beamformer and a larger C/N₀ buffer. A filter declares its combining weights rather than combining the taps itself, so the C/N₀ path can reduce the measured noise covariance through the same weights:\n\njulia> using Tracking, TrackingLoops, GNSSSignals, StaticArrays\n\njulia> using Tracking: Hz\n\njulia> using TrackingLoops: NumAnts, AbstractPostCorrFilter\n\njulia> # Trivial beamformer — averages across antenna elements\n       struct MyBeamformer <: AbstractPostCorrFilter end\n\njulia> TrackingLoops.update(f::MyBeamformer, prompt) = f;\n\njulia> TrackingLoops.get_weights(::MyBeamformer, ::NumAnts{1}) = 1.0 + 0.0im;\n\njulia> TrackingLoops.get_weights(::MyBeamformer, ::NumAnts{M}) where {M} =\n           SVector{M,ComplexF64}(ntuple(_ -> 1 / M + 0.0im, M));\n\njulia> sat = TrackedSat(GPSL1CA(), 1, 50.0, 1000.0Hz;\n           num_ants                       = NumAnts(4),\n           correlator                     = EarlyPromptLateCorrelator(\n               num_ants = NumAnts(4),\n               preferred_early_late_to_prompt_code_shift = 0.1,\n           ),\n           post_corr_filter               = MyBeamformer(),\n           num_prompts_for_cn0_estimation = 200,\n       );\n\njulia> track_state = TrackState(GPSL1CA(), sat);\n\njulia> get_num_ants(track_state, 1)\n4\n\njulia> get_correlator(track_state, 1).preferred_early_late_to_prompt_code_shift\n0.1\n\nFor a multi-signal satellite, customize individual TrackedSignals with the kwarg-update constructor TrackedSat(sat; signals = (...)).","title":"Power-user: pre-built TrackedSats"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#Adding-satellites","page":"Tracking State","text":"add_satellite! builds a TrackedSat with the default correlator and post-correlation filter from the acquisition handoff (prn, code_phase, carrier_doppler, optionally code_doppler and carrier_phase). Adding a PRN again overwrites it, as merge_sats does.","title":"Adding satellites"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#Multi-satellite-tracking","page":"Tracking State","text":"julia> using Tracking, GNSSSignals\n\njulia> using Tracking: Hz\n\njulia> track_state = TrackState(; signal = GPSL1CA());\n\njulia> track_state = add_satellite!(track_state; prn = 1,  code_phase = 50.0,  carrier_doppler = 1000.0Hz);\n\njulia> track_state = add_satellite!(track_state; prn = 5,  code_phase = 120.0, carrier_doppler = -500.0Hz);\n\njulia> track_state = add_satellite!(track_state; prn = 17, code_phase = 890.0, carrier_doppler = 2000.0Hz);\n\njulia> get_carrier_doppler(track_state, 5)\n-500.0 Hz\n\njulia> get_code_phase(track_state, 17)\n890.0","title":"Multi-satellite tracking"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#Multi-system-tracking-(different-signals-on-different-sats)","page":"Tracking State","text":"Satellites with different signals go into different named groups:\n\njulia> using Tracking, GNSSSignals\n\njulia> using Tracking: Hz\n\njulia> track_state = TrackState(;\n           signals = (\n               gps     = (GPSL1CA(),),\n               galileo = (GalileoE1B(),),\n           ),\n       );\n\njulia> track_state = add_satellite!(track_state; prn = 1,  group = :gps,     code_phase = 50.0,  carrier_doppler = 1000.0Hz);\n\njulia> track_state = add_satellite!(track_state; prn = 11, group = :galileo, code_phase = 200.0, carrier_doppler = -300.0Hz);\n\njulia> get_carrier_doppler(track_state, :gps, 1)\n1000.0 Hz\n\njulia> get_carrier_doppler(track_state, :galileo, 11)\n-300.0 Hz","title":"Multi-system tracking (different signals on different sats)"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#Multi-signal-tracking-(one-satellite,-several-signals)","page":"Tracking State","text":"A modern GPS satellite transmits L1 C/A, L1C-D and L1C-P on one carrier; all three can be tracked together, sharing the carrier downconvert:\n\njulia> using Tracking, GNSSSignals\n\njulia> using Tracking: Hz\n\njulia> track_state = TrackState(;\n           signals = (\n               modern_gps = (GPSL1C_P(), GPSL1C_D(), GPSL1CA()),\n           ),\n       );\n\njulia> track_state = add_satellite!(track_state;\n           prn = 11, group = :modern_gps,\n           code_phase = 0.0, carrier_doppler = 1234.0Hz,\n       );\n\njulia> get_carrier_doppler(track_state, :modern_gps, 11)\n1234.0 Hz\n\nPut a pilot first where there is one: without data bits the PLL can integrate longer. The data signals still decode their bits, each with its own bit_buffer.\n\nSignals with different code periods (L1 C/A 1 ms, L1C-P 10 ms) each complete records on their own boundaries; the conventional estimators update the Doppler from the driver's records (see Chunked Doppler updates).","title":"Multi-signal tracking (one satellite, several signals)"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#Phased-array-tracking","page":"Tracking State","text":"Pass a Matrix measurement (rows = samples, columns = antennas) and declare the antenna count:\n\njulia> using Tracking, TrackingLoops, GNSSSignals\n\njulia> using Tracking: Hz\n\njulia> track_state = TrackState(;\n           signal = GPSL1CA(),\n           num_ants = NumAnts(4),\n       );\n\njulia> track_state = add_satellite!(track_state; prn = 1, code_phase = 50.0, carrier_doppler = 1000.0Hz);\n\njulia> get_num_ants(track_state, 1)\n4\n\nBy default the last antenna drives the discriminators. For a beamformer, build the TrackedSat with a custom post_corr_filter, whose get_weights are applied to the correlator and to the measured noise covariance alike, so the C/N₀ stays correct:\n\njulia> using Tracking, TrackingLoops, GNSSSignals, StaticArrays\n\njulia> using Tracking: Hz\n\njulia> using TrackingLoops: NumAnts, AbstractPostCorrFilter\n\njulia> # Same trivial mean-of-antennas filter as the power-user example above\n       struct MyBeamformer <: AbstractPostCorrFilter end\n\njulia> TrackingLoops.update(f::MyBeamformer, prompt) = f;\n\njulia> TrackingLoops.get_weights(::MyBeamformer, ::NumAnts{1}) = 1.0 + 0.0im;\n\njulia> TrackingLoops.get_weights(::MyBeamformer, ::NumAnts{M}) where {M} =\n           SVector{M,ComplexF64}(ntuple(_ -> 1 / M + 0.0im, M));\n\njulia> sat = TrackedSat(GPSL1CA(), 1, 50.0, 1000.0Hz;\n                        num_ants = NumAnts(4),\n                        post_corr_filter = MyBeamformer());\n\njulia> track_state = TrackState(GPSL1CA(), sat);\n\njulia> get_num_ants(track_state, 1)\n4","title":"Phased-array tracking"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#Acquisition-handoff","page":"Tracking State","text":"With using Acquisition, add_satellite! / add_satellite accept AcquisitionResults (or a vector of them, each routed independently). Without group =, an acq goes to the group whose longest-primary-code signal matches acq.system.\n\nusing Acquisition  # loads the extension\n\n# Single acq\nts = add_satellite!(ts, acq)                       # auto-route\nts = add_satellite!(ts, acq; group = :legacy_gps)  # explicit group, asserts match\n\n# Vector of acqs (mixed constellations OK)\nts = add_satellite!(ts, filter(is_detected, acqs))\n\nOnly the longest code's phase is unambiguous for the whole group, so hand over an L1C-P acq (not L1 C/A) for (GPSL1C_P(), GPSL1C_D(), GPSL1CA()).","title":"Acquisition handoff"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#Removing-satellites","page":"Tracking State","text":"julia> using Tracking, GNSSSignals\n\njulia> using Tracking: Hz\n\njulia> track_state = TrackState(; signal = GPSL1CA());\n\njulia> track_state = add_satellite!(track_state; prn = 1,  code_phase = 50.0,  carrier_doppler = 1000.0Hz);\n\njulia> track_state = add_satellite!(track_state; prn = 23, code_phase = 500.0, carrier_doppler = 1500.0Hz);\n\njulia> track_state = remove_satellite!(track_state; prn = 1);\n\njulia> haskey(get_sat_states(track_state, :default), 23)\ntrue\n\njulia> haskey(get_sat_states(track_state, :default), 1)\nfalse","title":"Removing satellites"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#Multi-band-tracking","page":"Tracking State","text":"Satellites broadcast on several RF bands (GPS L1 and L5, Galileo E1 and E5a), which arrive from separate front ends, generally at different sample rates. Each group of a multi-band TrackState sits on one band. Two bands give near-independent observations of one path: for ionosphere-free combinations, wider code bandwidth, and cross-band aiding, as the L1/L5 Doppler ratio is that of the carrier frequencies.\n\nTracking.jl provides per-band groups, measurement routing and an estimate phase that sees every band's records at once; for joint cross-band estimation see docs/plans/2026-05-15-multi-band-tracking-design.md.","title":"Multi-band tracking"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#Declaring-bands","page":"Tracking State","text":"A group's band is get_band(signals[1]):\n\njulia> using Tracking, GNSSSignals\n\njulia> track_state = TrackState(;\n           signals = (\n               legacy_gps_l1 = (GPSL1CA(),),\n               modern_gps_l1 = (GPSL1C_P(), GPSL1C_D(), GPSL1CA()),\n               galileo       = (GalileoE1B(),),\n               gps_l5        = (GPSL5I(),),\n           ),\n       );\n\njulia> keys(track_state.groups)\n(:legacy_gps_l1, :modern_gps_l1, :galileo, :gps_l5)\n\nThree groups on L1 (GPS L1 and Galileo E1 share the carrier), one on L5.","title":"Declaring bands"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking-against-multiple-measurements","page":"Tracking State","text":"Pass one BandMeasurement per band, in a NamedTuple keyed by GNSSSignals.get_band_id:\n\njulia> using Tracking, GNSSSignals\n\njulia> using Tracking: Hz\n\njulia> using GNSSSignals: gen_code, get_code_frequency, get_code_center_frequency_ratio\n\njulia> function make_signal(sys, prn, carrier_doppler, num_samples, fs)\n           code_freq = carrier_doppler * get_code_center_frequency_ratio(sys) + get_code_frequency(sys)\n           range = 0:num_samples-1\n           cis.(2π .* carrier_doppler .* range ./ fs) .*\n               gen_code(num_samples, sys, prn, fs, code_freq, 0.0)\n       end;\n\njulia> track_state = TrackState(;\n           signals = (legacy_gps_l1 = (GPSL1CA(),), gps_l5 = (GPSL5I(),)),\n       );\n\njulia> track_state = add_satellite!(track_state; prn = 1, group = :legacy_gps_l1, code_phase = 0.0, carrier_doppler = 200Hz);\n\njulia> track_state = add_satellite!(track_state; prn = 1, group = :gps_l5, code_phase = 0.0, carrier_doppler = -150Hz);\n\njulia> buf_l1 = make_signal(GPSL1CA(),  1, 200Hz,  4000,  4e6Hz);  # 1 ms at 4 MHz\n\njulia> buf_l5 = make_signal(GPSL5I(),   1, -150Hz, 25000, 25e6Hz); # 1 ms at 25 MHz\n\njulia> track!((L1 = BandMeasurement(buf_l1, 4e6Hz),\n               L5 = BandMeasurement(buf_l5, 25e6Hz)), track_state);\n\njulia> get_carrier_doppler(track_state, :legacy_gps_l1, 1)\n200.00000359633913 Hz\n\nget_band_id is nameof the band type, so user-defined bands have keys too. All measurements must cover exactly the same duration: 4000 samples at 4 MHz and 25000 at 25 MHz are both 1 ms; 25001 at 25 MHz is rejected.","title":"Tracking against multiple measurements"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#Per-band-antenna-counts","page":"Tracking State","text":"Pass SignalGroups as the entries to give each group its own num_ants:\n\njulia> using Tracking, TrackingLoops, GNSSSignals\n\njulia> using TrackingLoops: NumAnts\n\njulia> track_state = TrackState(;\n           signals = (\n               legacy_gps_l1 = SignalGroup((GPSL1CA(),); num_ants = NumAnts(2)),\n               gps_l5        = SignalGroup((GPSL5I(),);  num_ants = NumAnts(1)),\n           ),\n       );\n\njulia> track_state.groups[:legacy_gps_l1].num_ants\nNumAnts{2}()\n\njulia> track_state.groups[:gps_l5].num_ants\nNumAnts{1}()\n\nGroups on one band share a front end and must declare the same num_ants; the constructor throws otherwise.\n\nA single-band TrackState can still be tracked with a bare buffer, track!(buf, state, fs; intermediate_frequency).","title":"Per-band antenna counts"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#SignalGroup","page":"Tracking State","text":"","title":"SignalGroup"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#Band-routing","page":"Tracking State","text":"band_keys lists the band ids a TrackState expects as measurement keys.","title":"Band routing"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#Addressing-satellites-and-signals","page":"Tracking State","text":"track_state.groups[:legacy_gps].satellites reaches a group directly. The accessors take the group key as an argument; their argument count says where the lookup stops:\n\nForm Meaning\nf(track_state) Single-group, single-sat — folds via only(...) at each level.\nf(track_state, prn) Single-group, multi-sat — picks the sat by PRN.\nf(track_state, group, prn) Multi-group — picks the sat in the named group.\nf(track_state, group, prn, sig) Per-signal — picks one TrackedSignal within a multi-signal sat.\n\nThe trailing sig selector is either:\n\nan Integer index into signals (1 is the estimator-driver signal), or\na signal type like GPSL1CA (not GPSL1CA()), which must match exactly one signal.","title":"Addressing satellites and signals"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#What-you-can-read","page":"Tracking State","text":"Sat-level — shared across all signals on the sat (no sig selector):\n\nAccessor Returns\nget_prn PRN number.\nget_num_ants Number of antenna elements.\nget_code_phase Shared code phase (wraps at max_code_length).\nget_code_doppler Shared code Doppler.\nget_carrier_phase Shared carrier phase in radians.\nget_carrier_doppler Shared carrier Doppler.\nget_signal_start_sample Index of the next sample to integrate.\n\nPer-signal — pass sig (index or type) on multi-signal sats:\n\nAccessor Returns\nget_correlator The working correlator (in-flight accumulator).\nget_last_fully_integrated_correlator Correlator value from the last completed integration.\nget_last_fully_integrated_filtered_prompt Filtered prompt value from the last completed integration.\nget_last_fully_integrated_num_code_blocks Primary-code block count of the last completed integration.\nget_last_fully_integrated_integration_time Integration time T of the last completed integration; the post-integration SNR is C/N₀ × T.\nget_post_corr_filter The post-correlation filter.\nget_cn0_estimator The CN0 estimator.\nestimate_cn0 CN0 estimate in dB-Hz.\nget_bit_buffer / get_soft_bits / get_num_bits Bit buffer, decoded soft bits (sign = the hard bit), bit count.\nget_integrated_samples Number of samples accumulated into the current integration so far.\nhas_bit_or_secondary_code_been_found true once bit/secondary-code synchronization has been achieved.\n\nThe per-signal form always names the group, :default on a single-group TrackState: estimate_cn0(track_state, :default, 11, GPSL1C_P).\n\njulia> using Tracking, GNSSSignals\n\njulia> using Tracking: Hz\n\njulia> track_state = TrackState(;\n           signals = (modern_gps = (GPSL1C_P(), GPSL1C_D(), GPSL1CA()),),\n       );\n\njulia> track_state = add_satellite!(track_state; prn = 11, group = :modern_gps,\n                                   code_phase = 0.0, carrier_doppler = 1234.0Hz);\n\njulia> get_carrier_doppler(track_state, :modern_gps, 11)  # sat-level: same for all signals\n1234.0 Hz\n\njulia> get_num_bits(track_state, :modern_gps, 11, 1)  # per-signal by index (estimator-driver signal)\n0\n\njulia> get_num_bits(track_state, :modern_gps, 11, GPSL1CA)  # per-signal by type\n0","title":"What you can read"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#Group-accessors","page":"Tracking State","text":"","title":"Group accessors"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#TrackedSat","page":"Tracking State","text":"The sat-level accessors also take a TrackedSat:","title":"TrackedSat"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#TrackedSignal","page":"Tracking State","text":"The per-signal accessors also take a TrackedSignal, as do get_signal(tsig), the signal instance, and get_filtered_prompts(tsig), the filtered prompts of the most recent track call.\n\nBit and secondary-code sync, and how it widens the code-phase wrap, have their own page: Bit and Secondary-Code Sync. Most users only need has_bit_or_secondary_code_been_found to gate get_soft_bits.","title":"TrackedSignal"},{"category":"section","location":"tracking_state.html#Handing-over-to-PositionVelocityTime","page":"Tracking State","text":"With the navigation data GNSSDecoder.jl decodes from their soft bits, tracked satellites become the measurements PositionVelocityTime.calc_pvt solves. Pass a NamedTuple of per-group PRN → GNSSDecoderState mappings:\n\nusing PositionVelocityTime: PositionVelocityTime, calc_pvt\npvt = calc_pvt(PositionVelocityTime.signal_groups(track_state, decoders))","title":"Handing over to PositionVelocityTime"},{"category":"type","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking.TrackState","page":"Tracking State","text":"The tracking state: the satellites of every signal group and the Doppler estimator.\n\ngroups is a NamedTuple of SignalGroups.\n\nnoise_estimators is a NamedTuple of AbstractNoiseEstimators keyed by signal id (GNSSSignals.get_signal_id), since the post-correlation floor depends on the despreading modulation. Only signals whose C/N₀ estimator reads a noise density (see requires_noise_density) get one, so the others pay nothing. They average in place.\n\nnoise_descriptor is per-call scratch: a heap cell where the correlate step parks the chunk's noise descriptors, so a threaded backend reaches them through one pointer (see _park_noise_items!). Shared by every derived TrackState.\n\nsample_offsets counts, per band (keyed by get_band_id), the samples earlier track! calls consumed: the origin of the current measurement on a sample grid that runs on across calls. The estimate phase adds it to a record's index, so an estimator with per-satellite state of its own (TrackingLoops' VectorPLLAndDLL) sees every satellite on one time grid. The in-place mutators share it; track, merge_sats, add_satellite and remove_satellite give their result a copy.\n\nA fresh TrackState starts every band at 0. To carry such an estimator over into a rebuilt TrackState (e.g. to add a band), pass the old state's offsets as the sample_offsets keyword, or the estimator receives records from its past. A band the old state did not track starts at elapsed_time * sampling_frequency samples of the new band.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.TrackState"},{"category":"function","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking.add_satellite!","page":"Tracking State","text":"add_satellite!(track_state; prn, group, kwargs...)\n\n\nAdd (or replace, by prn) a satellite in track_state in place. Builds a TrackedSat for the group with the default correlator and post-corr filter from the acquisition handoff (prn, code_phase, code_doppler, carrier_phase, carrier_doppler), its estimator state from init_estimator_state.\n\ngroup may be omitted on a single-group TrackState; otherwise an ArgumentError names the available groups.\n\nKeep using the returned TrackState: an estimator whose update_estimator_on_handoff returns a rebuilt estimator can only hand it back there (TrackState is immutable). Estimators that update in place, the conventional ones included, return the same track_state.\n\ntrack_state = TrackState(; signals = (modern_gps = (GPSL1C_P(), GPSL1C_D(), GPSL1CA()),))\nadd_satellite!(\n    track_state;\n    prn = 11,\n    group = :modern_gps,\n    code_phase = 0.0,\n    carrier_doppler = 1234.0Hz,\n)\n\nFor non-default correlator or post-corr-filter types, build the TrackState from such TrackedSats and add more with the add_satellite!(track_state, group, sat) overload.\n\n\n\n\n\nadd_satellite!(track_state, group, sat)\n\n\nIn-place add (or replace) with a pre-built TrackedSat. Its type must match the group's slot type fixed at TrackState construction, so non-default correlator or post-corr-filter types need a state built from such sats. Keep using the return value, as for the keyword form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.add_satellite!"},{"category":"function","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking.add_satellite","page":"Tracking State","text":"add_satellite(track_state; prn, group, kwargs...)\n\n\nImmutable variant of add_satellite!. Returns a new TrackState with the satellite added; the input is left unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.add_satellite"},{"category":"function","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking.remove_satellite!","page":"Tracking State","text":"remove_satellite!(track_state; prn, group)\n\n\nRemove a satellite from track_state in place; a KeyError if the group has no such prn.\n\nremove_satellite!(track_state; prn = 11, group = :modern_gps)\n\ngroup may be omitted as for add_satellite!. Returns track_state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.remove_satellite!"},{"category":"function","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking.remove_satellite","page":"Tracking State","text":"remove_satellite(track_state; prn, group)\n\n\nImmutable variant of remove_satellite!. Returns a new TrackState with the satellite removed; the input is left unchanged. Errors if no satellite with the given prn exists.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.remove_satellite"},{"category":"function","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking.merge_sats","page":"Tracking State","text":"merge_sats(track_state, group_idx, tracked_sats)\n\n\nMerge already-built TrackedSats into the group_idx group of track_state, returning a new TrackState (the input is left unchanged). tracked_sats may be a single TrackedSat, a Vector, or a Dictionary keyed by PRN; existing PRNs in the group are overwritten.\n\nEach sat's type must match the group's slot type, including the doppler_estimator_state the configured estimator produces. The estimator's update_estimator_on_handoff hook is called once with the incoming sats.\n\nFor a single-group TrackState the group_idx may be omitted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.merge_sats"},{"category":"type","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking.SignalGroup","page":"Tracking State","text":"A group of satellites tracking the same tuple of signals, on one RF band and antenna array: the unit of type stability (one concrete TrackedSat type). Groups may share a band (e.g. (GPSL1CA(),) and (GalileoE1B(),) on L1()); the band routes the right measurement to the group.\n\nFields:\n\nband: an AbstractGNSSSignal Band instance (L1(), L5(), …)\nsatellites: Dictionary{Int, <:TrackedSat} keyed by PRN\nsignals: the signal-instance tuple (e.g. (GPSL1C_P(), GPSL1C_D(), GPSL1CA()))\nnum_ants: the antenna count for this group's band\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.SignalGroup"},{"category":"type","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking.SignalGroups","page":"Tracking State","text":"A NamedTuple of N SignalGroups, as stored in TrackState.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.SignalGroups"},{"category":"function","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking.band_keys","page":"Tracking State","text":"band_keys(track_state)\n\n\nThe distinct band ids (GNSSSignals.get_band_id) of track_state's groups, in first-encounter order: the keys a multi-band measurements NamedTuple must use.\n\nts = TrackState(; signals = (legacy_gps = (GPSL1CA(),), gps_l5 = (GPSL5I(),)))\nband_keys(ts) == (:L1, :L5)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.band_keys"},{"category":"type","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking.SatelliteDicts","page":"Tracking State","text":"A tuple or named tuple of per-group satellite dictionaries, each a Dictionary{I, <:TrackedSat} keyed by PRN.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.SatelliteDicts"},{"category":"function","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking.get_sat_states","page":"Tracking State","text":"get_sat_states(satellites, group_idx)\n\n\nGet the per-group satellite dictionary for a specific signal-group index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.get_sat_states"},{"category":"function","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking.get_sat_state","page":"Tracking State","text":"get_sat_state(sats, identifier)\n\n\nGet the satellite state for a specific satellite identifier.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.get_sat_state"},{"category":"function","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking.get_signal","page":"Tracking State","text":"get_signal(track_state, group_idx)\n\n\nReturn the first (driver) signal of the given group, also for a group without satellites. Resolves at compile time for a literal group_idx.\n\nFor a single-group TrackState the index can be omitted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.get_signal"},{"category":"type","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking.TrackedSat","page":"Tracking State","text":"The state of one tracked satellite: the carrier/code Doppler and phase shared by its signals, the per-signal state in signals, and the Doppler-estimator state in doppler_estimator_state.\n\nsignals[1] is the estimator-driver signal. The conventional estimators (ConventionalPLLAndDLL, ConventionalAssistedPLLAndDLL) run their discriminators on it, at its integration boundaries; the other signals update their own filters, C/N₀ and bit buffers on theirs. A custom AbstractDopplerEstimator may use any signal.\n\nThe shared code_phase wraps at max_code_length.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.TrackedSat"},{"category":"method","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking.get_prn-Tuple{TrackedSat}","page":"Tracking State","text":"get_prn(s)\n\n\nGet the PRN (Pseudo-Random Noise) number of the satellite.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.get_prn"},{"category":"method","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking.get_code_phase-Tuple{TrackedSat}","page":"Tracking State","text":"get_code_phase(s)\n\n\nGet the satellite's shared code phase in chips, wrapping at max_code_length. Mod by a signal's primary code length for its replica phase.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.get_code_phase"},{"category":"method","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking.get_code_doppler-Tuple{TrackedSat}","page":"Tracking State","text":"get_code_doppler(s)\n\n\nGet the current code Doppler frequency.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.get_code_doppler"},{"category":"method","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking.get_carrier_phase-Tuple{TrackedSat}","page":"Tracking State","text":"get_carrier_phase(s)\n\n\nGet the current carrier phase in radians.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.get_carrier_phase"},{"category":"method","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking.get_carrier_doppler-Tuple{TrackedSat}","page":"Tracking State","text":"get_carrier_doppler(s)\n\n\nGet the current carrier Doppler frequency.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.get_carrier_doppler"},{"category":"method","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking.get_signal_start_sample-Tuple{TrackedSat}","page":"Tracking State","text":"get_signal_start_sample(s)\n\n\nGet the starting sample index in the signal for the next integration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.get_signal_start_sample"},{"category":"method","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking.get_signals-Tuple{TrackedSat}","page":"Tracking State","text":"get_signals(s)\n\n\nGet the satellite's tuple of TrackedSignals.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.get_signals"},{"category":"method","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking.get_doppler_estimator_state-Tuple{TrackedSat}","page":"Tracking State","text":"get_doppler_estimator_state(s)\n\n\nGet the per-satellite Doppler estimator state (e.g. the loop-filter state for the conventional PLL/DLL).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.get_doppler_estimator_state"},{"category":"type","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking.TrackedSignal","page":"Tracking State","text":"Per-signal tracking state, one per signal tracked on a satellite: the correlator, the integration progress and the per-record loop state, a TrackingLoops SignalLoopState (post-corr filter, C/N₀ estimator, bit buffer, and what the last record left for the next). The carrier/code Doppler and phase are shared and live on the TrackedSat.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.TrackedSignal"},{"category":"method","location":"tracking_state.html#Tracking.get_last_fully_integrated_integration_time-Tuple{TrackedSignal}","page":"Tracking State","text":"get_last_fully_integrated_integration_time(t)\n\n\nThe integration time T of the most recently completed record (its primary-code blocks times one code period), which estimate_cn0 divides by. The record's post-integration SNR is C/N₀ · T.\n\nNot to be confused with get_integrated_samples, the record in progress.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.get_last_fully_integrated_integration_time"},{"category":"method","location":"tracking_state.html#PositionVelocityTime.signal_groups-Tuple{TrackState, NamedTuple}","page":"Tracking State","text":"PositionVelocityTime.signal_groups(track_state::TrackState, decoders)\n    -> NamedTuple of PositionVelocityTime.SignalGroup\n\nOne epoch's measurements from a whole TrackState for PositionVelocityTime.calc_pvt. decoders has the group keys of track_state.groups, each a PRN → GNSSDecoderState mapping; both levels of key are kept. Each group ranges on its first signal (see PositionVelocityTime.SignalGroup(::Tracking.SignalGroup, decoders)). Inferable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PositionVelocityTime.signal_groups"},{"category":"method","location":"tracking_state.html#PositionVelocityTime.SignalGroup-Tuple{SignalGroup, Any}","page":"Tracking State","text":"PositionVelocityTime.SignalGroup(group::Tracking.SignalGroup, decoders;\n                                 signal = first(group.signals))\n\nOne Tracking.SignalGroup's measurements for an epoch, each satellite paired with its decoded navigation data.\n\ndecoders maps the group's PRNs to GNSSDecoderStates (a Dictionary, or any getindex/haskey mapping); the result keeps those keys. Satellites without a decoder are skipped, as tracking starts long before the message is complete.\n\nsignal is the ranging signal, by default the group's first (the estimator driver, and GNSSReceiver's RANGING_SIGNAL_INDEX = 1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PositionVelocityTime.SignalGroup"},{"category":"method","location":"tracking_state.html#PositionVelocityTime.SatelliteState-Tuple{GNSSDecoder.GNSSDecoderState, AbstractGNSSSignal, TrackedSat}","page":"Tracking State","text":"PositionVelocityTime.SatelliteState(decoder::GNSSDecoderState, system, sat_state::TrackedSat)\n\nOne tracked satellite's code phase, carrier Doppler and carrier phase (from get_sat_state) paired with its decoded navigation data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PositionVelocityTime.SatelliteState"},{"category":"section","location":"noise_estimator.html#Noise-Estimator","page":"Noise Estimator","text":"NoiseRefCN0Estimator, the default C/N₀ estimator, divides each record's prompt power by a measured noise floor. This page is about where that floor comes from.\n\nThe floor is estimated once per signal, shared by every satellite on it, which makes the reference's own variance negligible. The estimators live in TrackState's noise_estimators field, a NamedTuple keyed by signal id (GNSSSignals.get_signal_id: :GPSL1CA, :GalileoE1B, …).\n\nOn the sample-driven path nothing needs configuring: TrackState provisions a CorrelatorNoiseEstimator for every signal asking for a density, and track! fills it. Read on if you feed correlator outputs from hardware.\n\nIn the TrackingLoops manual:\n\nAbstractNoiseEstimator","title":"Noise Estimator"},{"category":"section","location":"noise_estimator.html#Why-per-signal-and-not-per-RF-band","page":"Noise Estimator","text":"What a record divides by is the post-correlation floor, weighted by the despreading modulation's own spectrum, so two signals on one front end see different floors as soon as the interference is not white (see AbstractNoiseEstimator). Measured through track! on one shared set of samples, 13 dB J/N tone, densities relative to the thermal floor (4 MHz, pure BOC(1,1) E1B):\n\ntone GPS L1 C/A Galileo E1B E1B / C/A\nnone 1.0 1.0 1.0\n1.023 MHz 1.4 39 28×\n0.4 MHz 35 26 0.75×\n\nReporting C/A's figure to E1B in the middle row overstates E1B's C/N₀ by ≈14.5 dB; the reversal in the last row makes this a spectral effect, not a scale factor. The real CBOC E1B shows the same reversal about four times larger. Despreading with the consumer's own code measures this weighting rather than modelling it.\n\nThe samples remain a band property (BandMeasurements is keyed by band id); only the despread, and so the floor, is per signal.","title":"Why per signal and not per RF band"},{"category":"section","location":"noise_estimator.html#Why-a-density-and-not-a-power","page":"Noise Estimator","text":"With Tracking's normalisation, white noise gives E|P|² = N₀/T, so N₀ = σ²/f_s is independent of the integration time (see AbstractNoiseEstimator). One per-signal figure therefore serves a satellite on 1-block pre-sync records and one on 20-block post-sync records alike, and the reference needs no alignment to correlator records: it runs on any grid and averages over a much longer window than any prompt buffer. N₀ carries Unitful dimensions of 1/Hz, so the arithmetic is dimension-checked.","title":"Why a density and not a power"},{"category":"section","location":"noise_estimator.html#The-two-fill-paths","page":"Noise Estimator","text":" software hardware\nfills the window via track! → downconvert_and_correlate! → update_noise! append_noise_observation!\nenters downconvert_and_correlate!? yes no\nneeds sample buffers? yes no\n\nThe paths never meet (a hardware producer never calls downconvert_and_correlate!), so you configure CorrelatorNoiseEstimator either way and only fill it differently.\n\nIn the TrackingLoops manual:\n\nupdate_noise!\nget_noise_density","title":"The two fill paths"},{"category":"section","location":"noise_estimator.html#The-software-source","page":"Noise Estimator","text":"In the TrackingLoops manual:\n\nCorrelatorNoiseEstimator\nCorrelatorNoiseEstimator()\nupdate_noise!(::CorrelatorNoiseEstimator, …)\n\nIts design is documented on CorrelatorNoiseEstimator: it is open-loop and reads no satellite state, so it works with zero satellites tracked; its code phase and carrier are randomised per sub-integration, so a chance alignment with an untracked signal is a one-observation outlier rather than a standing bias, which also lets the PRN rotate over the whole family; its sub-integration length is the primary code period, derived, not configured, with the variance bought back by window_duration.\n\nIt is model-free: the reference runs the prompt's own quantise → downconvert → despread → accumulate path on the same kernel, so N̂₀ already contains that chain's losses with no per-backend correction. The same 45 dB-Hz sky reports ≈45 dB-Hz on the float and Int16 backends, ≈44 on two-bit and ≈43 on one-bit: each backend's own quantisation loss.","title":"The software source"},{"category":"section","location":"noise_estimator.html#Hardware-producers-(FPGA-/-ASIC)","page":"Noise Estimator","text":"Reuse the downconvert-and-correlate datapath: allocate a channel. On the architectures FPGA correlators use, that is also the cheaper option:\n\nOn a time-multiplexed engine (one datapath across N channels, per-channel state in BRAM) a noise channel is one more slot in the schedule; a dedicated path is a second datapath.\nOn a fully parallel array it costs less than a tracking channel, being open-loop: fixed NCO increments, no discriminator, no loop-filter interface, no dump-on-command synchronisation. A dedicated path would re-implement the carrier NCO, code generator and complex multiplier.\nNo divider and no float on chip. Report the raw integer accumulation with its weight and sample count; all division, A_c² and f_s scaling and averaging happen in Tracking.\n\nReuse is also what makes the measurement model-free: identical quantisation (the 1-bit/2-bit loss cancels in the ratio), identical input scaling (an AGC step moves numerator and denominator together), identical code generator (A_c matches). A path tapping at a different point, before a decimation filter or at a different bit width, breaks all three silently.","title":"Hardware producers (FPGA / ASIC)"},{"category":"section","location":"noise_estimator.html#What-the-producer-must-match","page":"Noise Estimator","text":"Report raw accumulations through one of the builders below, on the correlator outputs' return path: append_noise_observation! sits beside append_correlator_output! on the host.\nRandomise the code phase per dump, and rotate the PRN (a free-running code generator does this; phase 0 would need a reset). Dithering the carrier a few kHz around the nominal IF strengthens it further. The noise channel then needs no input from the tracking channels.\n\nA producer that dumps on the code epoch matches the software default exactly. One that differs still lands in the same M-weighted, time-bounded window, on the same scale; only the precision differs, and K_n ≈ 1000 looks per second per tap is indistinguishable from anything finer below 40 dB-Hz.","title":"What the producer must match"},{"category":"section","location":"noise_estimator.html#The-observation-and-its-builders","page":"Noise Estimator","text":"In the TrackingLoops manual:\n\nNoiseObservation\nnoise_observation\nnoise_observation_from_correlator\nnoise_observation_from_samples\n\nAll three reduce to the same N₀ on the same white input. The weight is M, the number of independent looks, not the sample count: M sub-integrations give a relative variance of 1/M, so one 1 ms despread and 64 16-chip despreads cover the same samples at very different precision. Weighting by M makes observations from different producers combinable.\n\nA worked ingest, per fold:\n\n# taps, per signal, exactly as before\nappend_correlator_output!(track_state, CorrelatorOutput(correlator, n, sample_index), prn)\n\n# the noise channel, per signal: `accumulated_power` is the raw Σ|B|² pooled over\n# `num_taps` accumulators, so the sample count is per tap times that\nappend_noise_observation!(\n    track_state,\n    noise_observation_from_correlator(\n        accumulated_power,\n        num_taps,\n        num_taps * samples_per_dump,\n        sampling_frequency;\n        prn = noise_prn,\n        # the taps integrate the same samples, so the span counts once\n        duration = samples_per_dump / sampling_frequency,\n    ),\n    :GPSL1CA,\n)\n\nestimate_dopplers_and_filter_prompt!(track_state, (L1 = sampling_frequency,))\n\nIf a signal's noise estimator is never filled, the fold warns once per signal and every C/N₀ on it reads -Inf dB-Hz.","title":"The observation and its builders"},{"category":"section","location":"noise_estimator.html#How-append_noise_observation!-differs-from-append_correlator_output!","page":"Noise Estimator","text":" append_correlator_output! append_noise_observation!\nscope one signal of one satellite one signal, across every satellite tracking it\npayload the whole correlator: every tap kept separate, complex, per antenna the taps pooled — into one power sum, or into one spatial covariance for an array — plus M, the span and the PRN\ndrives discriminators, both loop filters, bit sync, the NCO update, the C/N₀ prompt the noise floor only\ntiming sample_index is load-bearing — vector tracking needs a common grid none: N₀ is slowly varying, so only \"recent\" matters\nlifetime drained by the fold each chunk, buffer reused sliding window bounded in time, never drained\ncount one per completed coherent integration per signal any granularity; M carries the weight\n\nThe prompt path keeps the taps apart because their differences are the discriminants; the reference pools them, as at ≥1 chip spacing they are independent looks at the same noise.\n\nIt keeps the antenna dimension: with num_ants > 1 the payload is the M×M spatial covariance R̂ = Σ_k b_k·b_kᴴ, since a beamformer's floor is wᴴR̂w under its own weights. Every builder takes the per-antenna form of its input.","title":"How append_noise_observation! differs from append_correlator_output!"},{"category":"section","location":"noise_estimator.html#Writing-your-own-source","page":"Noise Estimator","text":"A source that is neither shipped path (a front-end power monitor, say) subtypes AbstractNoiseEstimator:\n\nstruct MyPowerMonitor <: TrackingLoops.AbstractNoiseEstimator\n    densities::Vector{typeof(1.0 / 1.0Hz)}\nend\n\n# fed from outside; `update_noise!` defaults to a no-op\nTrackingLoops.append_noise_observation!(e::MyPowerMonitor, obs) =\n    (push!(e.densities, obs.noise_density); e)\nTrackingLoops.get_noise_density(e::MyPowerMonitor) =\n    isempty(e.densities) ? nothing : sum(e.densities) / length(e.densities)\n\nPass it in through TrackState's noise_estimators keyword:\n\nTrackState(; signal = GPSL1CA(), noise_estimators = (GPSL1CA = MyPowerMonitor(...),))\n\nTwo contracts: mutate the window in place and return the struct (TrackState is never rebuilt for a noise update), and have TrackingLoops.noise_density_type name the concrete type get_noise_density returns (default typeof(1.0/1.0Hz)), so the fold stays monomorphic.\n\nIn the TrackingLoops manual:\n\nnoise_density_type\nNoiseUpdateContext\nrequires_noise_density","title":"Writing your own source"},{"category":"function","location":"noise_estimator.html#TrackingLoops.append_noise_observation!","page":"Noise Estimator","text":"append_noise_observation!(\n    track_state,\n    observation,\n    signal_id\n)\n\n\nAppend an externally built NoiseObservation to the addressed signal's noise estimator and return track_state:\n\nappend_noise_observation!(track_state, obs) — single-signal TrackState.\nappend_noise_observation!(track_state, obs, :GPSL1CA) — explicit signal id.\nappend_noise_observation!(track_state, obs, GPSL1CA) — or the signal type / an instance of it, which is what a caller usually has to hand.\n\nThe hardware/FPGA fill path: append_correlator_output! and append_noise_observation! per signal, then estimate_dopplers_and_filter_prompt! to fold (deliberately separate mechanisms; see the estimator-level append_noise_observation! for how they differ). Per signal, not per band, because the post-correlation floor depends on the despreading modulation: a noise channel is a tracking channel with a wrong PRN (see AbstractNoiseEstimator).\n\nThe signal must have a noise estimator: provisioned when its C/N₀ estimator reads a density (see requires_noise_density), or declared through TrackState's noise_estimators keyword.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TrackingLoops.append_noise_observation!"},{"category":"function","location":"noise_estimator.html#TrackingLoops.despread_noise!","page":"Noise Estimator","text":"despread_noise!(\n    backend,\n    estimator,\n    measurement,\n    first_sample,\n    last_sample,\n    context\n)\n\n\nMeasure this signal's noise over samples first_sample:last_sample of measurement and append the resulting observations, returning estimator.\n\nThe slice is split into num_sub equal sub-integrations,\n\nnum_sub = max(1, round(Int, slice_duration / code_period))\n\nwith code_period the primary code period of the signal being measured (see CorrelatorNoiseEstimator for why it is derived, not configured). Equal slices waste no remainder; the max(1, …) keeps a code longer than the chunk (10 ms code, 1 ms chunks) from never yielding an observation. Each observation is pushed individually, so the window's time span does not follow the Doppler update rate.\n\nEach sub-integration draws a fresh random code phase and a fresh random carrier offset within ±carrier_dither of the band's IF, so every window entry is an independent trial (see CorrelatorNoiseEstimator). It follows no code-period boundary: with a wrong PRN at a wrong phase there is nothing to accumulate coherently, so N̂₀ = |B|²/(N·A_c²·f_s) is unbiased for any N and start phase, and no partial accumulator has to be carried across calls.\n\nAll taps, tap_code_shift chips apart, are pooled into one power sum, despread on the caller's backend kernel (the one the prompt goes through), which keeps the measurement model-free. With num_ants > 1 every antenna column is despread and the taps pool into the spatial covariance R̂ = Σ_k b_k·b_kᴴ; each satellite reduces it to its own floor wᴴR̂w at C/N₀ time.\n\nThe reference is open-loop and reads no satellite state. The PRN rotates over the whole family, tracked or not; the random phase makes avoiding tracked ones unnecessary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TrackingLoops.despread_noise!"},{"category":"section","location":"signals.html#Signal-support-matrix","page":"Signal support matrix","text":"Every concrete signal type GNSSSignals.jl defines, and what Tracking.jl does with it. test/signal_coverage.jl checks the table against the live type tree, so a GNSSSignals release that adds a signal fails the test suite rather than user code.","title":"Signal support matrix"},{"category":"section","location":"signals.html#What-\"supported\"-means-here","page":"Signal support matrix","text":"Tracking is not navigation decoding, and neither is PVT.\n\nSync feature is what the tracker locks onto to leave its one-block pre-sync integration: a data-bit edge, a secondary-code phase, or nothing where the primary code period already is the integration unit.\nNavigation data is what the signal carries. A pilot or acquisition aid carries none: it yields code phase, carrier phase, Doppler and C/N₀, and get_soft_bits stays empty. Decoding is GNSSDecoder.jl's job, and a satellite can be ranged on without it.\n\nEvidence level. Every row is verified against clean synthetic GNSSSignals replicas: TrackingLoops' test/signals.jl pins defaults and sync behaviour, and test/signal_coverage.jl here runs each signal through track. Simulated-FPGA, hardware-replay and live-RF validation are tracked in GNSSReceiver.jl#130.","title":"What \"supported\" means here"},{"category":"section","location":"signals.html#The-matrix","page":"Signal support matrix","text":"Blocks per integration is the coherent integration length in primary code blocks a fresh TrackedSignal starts at (default_num_code_blocks_to_integrate), and in brackets the ceiling (max_num_code_blocks_to_integrate) set_preferred_num_code_blocks_to_integrate! may raise it to: one data bit, overlay period or code cycle. Until sync, one block is integrated regardless.\n\nSignal Band Primary code Blocks per integration Sync feature Navigation data PRNs\nGPSL1CA L1 1023 chips, 1 ms 1 (max 20) soft CFAR bit-edge search LNAV, 50 sym/s 1-37\nGPSL1C_D L1 10230 chips, 10 ms 1 (max 1) none needed — 1 symbol per code period CNAV-2, 100 sym/s 1-63\nGPSL1C_P L1 10230 chips, 10 ms 1 (max 1800) hard rotation sweep, 1800-chip overlay none (pilot) 1-63\nGPSL2CM L2 10230 chips, 20 ms 1 (max 1) none needed — 1 symbol per code period CNAV, 50 sym/s 1-63\nGPSL2CL L2 767250 chips, 1.5 s 1 (max 1) none — the code period is the integration none (pilot) 1-63\nGPSL5I L5 10230 chips, 1 ms 1 (max 10) soft CFAR overlay search, NH10 CNAV, 100 sym/s 1-37\nGPSL5Q L5 10230 chips, 1 ms 1 (max 20) soft CFAR overlay search, NH20 none (pilot) 1-37\nGalileoE1B L1 4092 chips, 4 ms 1 (max 1) none needed — 1 symbol per code period I/NAV, 250 sym/s 1-50\nGalileoE1B_BOC11 L1 4092 chips, 4 ms 1 (max 1) none needed — 1 symbol per code period I/NAV, 250 sym/s 1-50\nGalileoE1C L1 4092 chips, 4 ms 1 (max 25) soft CFAR overlay search, CS25 none (pilot) 1-50\nGalileoE1C_BOC11 L1 4092 chips, 4 ms 1 (max 25) soft CFAR overlay search, CS25 none (pilot) 1-50\nGalileoE5aI L5 10230 chips, 1 ms 1 (max 20) soft CFAR overlay search, CS20 F/NAV, 50 sym/s 1-50\nGalileoE5aQ L5 10230 chips, 1 ms 1 (max 100) soft CFAR overlay search, per-PRN CS100 none (pilot) 1-50\nGalileoE5aQP L5 330 chips, 64.5 µs 31 (max 31) none — no data, no overlay none (acquisition aid) 1-40\nGalileoE5bI E5b 10230 chips, 1 ms 1 (max 4) soft CFAR overlay search, CS4 I/NAV, 250 sym/s 1-50\nGalileoE5bQ E5b 10230 chips, 1 ms 1 (max 100) soft CFAR overlay search, per-SVID CS100 none (pilot) 1-50\nGalileoE6B E6 5115 chips, 1 ms 1 (max 1) none needed — 1 symbol per code period C/NAV, 1000 sym/s 1-50\nGalileoE6C E6 5115 chips, 1 ms 1 (max 100) soft CFAR overlay search, per-PRN CS100 none (pilot) 1-50\nBeiDouB1I B1I 2046 chips, 1 ms 1 (max 20) soft CFAR overlay search, NH20 D1, 50 sym/s (see GEO note) 1-63\nBeiDouB3I B3I 10230 chips, 1 ms 1 (max 20) soft CFAR overlay search, NH20 D1, 50 sym/s (see GEO note) 1-63\nBeiDouB1C_D L1 10230 chips, 10 ms 1 (max 1) none needed — 1 symbol per code period B-CNAV1, 100 sym/s 1-63\nBeiDouB1C_P L1 10230 chips, 10 ms 1 (max 1800) hard rotation sweep, 1800-chip overlay none (pilot) 1-63\nBeiDouB2aI L5 10230 chips, 1 ms 1 (max 5) soft CFAR overlay search, 5-chip B-CNAV2, 200 sym/s 1-63\nBeiDouB2aQ L5 10230 chips, 1 ms 1 (max 100) soft CFAR overlay search, per-PRN 100-chip none (pilot) 1-63\nBeiDouB2bI E5b 10230 chips, 1 ms 1 (max 1) none needed — 1 symbol per code period B-CNAV3, 1000 sym/s (see below) 6-58\n\nPRNs is the range the upstream code table defines; this package does not check it, and other PRNs give an all-zero or out-of-bounds code. BeiDou B2b-I's table has 63 columns, but the ICD defines codes for PRN 6-58 only.\n\nSignals sharing a band can be tracked in one BandMeasurement regardless of constellation: Galileo E5b and BeiDou B2b share the 1207.14 MHz carrier (E5b for both), BeiDou B2a shares L5 with GPS L5 and Galileo E5a, and BeiDou B1C shares L1 with GPS L1 C/A, GPS L1C and Galileo E1.","title":"The matrix"},{"category":"section","location":"signals.html#Galileo-E5a-QP,-the-acquisition-aid","page":"Signal support matrix","text":"E5a-QP (OS SIS ICD v2.2 §2.3.1.4) is a dataless BPSK(5) quasi-pilot at 5.115 Mcps with a 330-chip, 64.5 µs primary code, repeated 31 times per 2 ms with no overlay, broadcast so a receiver can find Galileo with a search 31× smaller than E5a-I's.\n\nAs an acquisition aid. Its carrier Doppler transfers directly to E5a-I/Q (same carrier). Its code phase pins E5a-I only modulo 64.5 µs, leaving 31 hypotheses, still a 330× reduction. At half the E5a-I/Q chip rate it cannot share their SignalGroup (#151); track it in its own group and hand over.\nAs a tracked signal it yields the usual observables. One 64.5 µs block would run the loop at 15.5 kHz, so it integrates a whole 31-block, 2 ms code cycle by default: a 500 Hz loop, where the carrier loop pulls in at 45 Hz and narrows to 18 Hz.\nIt never provides navigation data, for itself or for E5a.\n\nOnly part of the constellation transmits it (see the GSC list), and the ICD says it will eventually be discontinued.","title":"Galileo E5a-QP, the acquisition aid"},{"category":"section","location":"signals.html#Known-limitations","page":"Signal support matrix","text":"BeiDou GEO satellites (PRN 1-5, 59-63) never sync on B1I/B3I. They carry no NH20 overlay and broadcast D2 at 500 sym/s instead of D1 at 50 sym/s, so the secondary-code detector has nothing to lock onto. They track and can be ranged on, but are not decoded: a D2 bit-edge search would need a per-PRN data rate, while get_data_frequency reports D1's for every PRN. See TrackingLoops' src/beidou/b1i.jl.\nBeiDou B2b-I correlates to an all-zero prompt on a clean replica, which takes the loop down through a NaN Doppler (#234); its defaults and sync detector are checked, but it is not usable end to end.\nLong-overlay pilots (GPSL1C_P, BeiDouB1C_P) need a full 18 s overlay period before the hard rotation sweep can lock (#215).\nSecondary-code phase is re-searched at handoff even when the acquisition stage already recovered it (#120).\nOne chip rate per SignalGroup, which keeps E5a-QP out of an E5a group (#151).\nPilots without a secondary code (GPS L2 CL, Galileo E5a-QP) keep the two-quadrant Costas discriminators, at half the four-quadrant range, as no sync gives them the prompt sign; secondary-coded pilots switch to four-quadrant once synced (#230).","title":"Known limitations"},{"category":"section","location":"track.html#Track","page":"Track","text":"track takes a measurement and a TrackState, downconverts and correlates every tracked satellite, drives the Doppler estimator, and returns the updated TrackState. track! is the in-place counterpart for real-time loops; after one warmup call its single-threaded path is allocation-free (see track! for the threaded path's residual).","title":"Track"},{"category":"section","location":"track.html#Optional-parameters","page":"Track","text":"downconvert_and_correlator: the backend, by default CPUThreadedDownconvertAndCorrelator(). Hoist it outside real-time loops (see below).\nintermediate_frequency: the IF, by default 0.0Hz. Bare-buffer form only; otherwise it lives on each BandMeasurement.\ndoppler_update_interval: the NCO-update interval, a time (e.g. 1u\"ms\"). nothing means the smallest primary-code period of the tracked signals (1 ms for GPS L1 C/A); see Chunked Doppler updates. Longer intervals cost less estimation at a lower update rate.\n\nThe coherent-integration length is set per signal with set_preferred_num_code_blocks_to_integrate!. It starts at default_num_code_blocks_to_integrate (1, except a 31-block, 2 ms code cycle for Galileo E5a-QP, whose 64.5 µs code period is too short to run a loop on), is capped by max_num_code_blocks_to_integrate (one bit or secondary-code period), and takes effect only after bit or secondary-code sync.\n\nIn the TrackingLoops manual:\n\ndefault_num_code_blocks_to_integrate\nmax_num_code_blocks_to_integrate","title":"Optional parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"track.html#Chunked-Doppler-updates","page":"Track","text":"track / track! walk each measurement in chunks of doppler_update_interval, each with one correlate pass and one estimate:\n\nCorrelate each satellite up to its last completed integration boundary in the chunk, collecting every completed integration into the signal's correlator_outputs as a CorrelatorOutput tagged with its end sample. A 1 ms code in a 1 ms chunk yields 0, 1 or 2 outputs.\nEstimate: the Doppler estimator folds the outputs in order and writes every satellite's NCO once per chunk, at a common epoch.\n\nThe next chunk's pass starts at that boundary, so every integration runs at one NCO Doppler and each correction takes effect at the boundary where its integration completed, as with a per-code-period update. A final pass drains each satellite's trailing partial into its accumulator, carried into the next track! call. get_correlator_outputs reads a chunk's outputs before the estimate clears them.","title":"Chunked Doppler updates"},{"category":"section","location":"track.html#Real-time-loops","page":"Track","text":"A typical receiver loop builds the TrackState once, hoists the correlator outside the loop, and calls track! per chunk:\n\ntrack_state = TrackState(; signal = GPSL1CA())\ntrack_state = add_satellite!(track_state; prn = 1, code_phase = 0.0, carrier_doppler = 1000.0Hz)\n# ... more sats ...\n\ndc = CPUThreadedDownconvertAndCorrelator()  # hoist outside the loop\n\nwhile got_signal_chunk(rx)\n    chunk = read_chunk!(rx)\n    track!(chunk, track_state, sampling_frequency;\n           downconvert_and_correlator = dc)\n    # ... read per-sat state e.g. via get_sat_state(track_state, group, prn) ...\nend\n\nThe backends hold per-thread scratch buffers that grow on first use; the keyword's default rebuilds them on every call, for track and track! alike. track! writes into the existing satellite slots, and from its second call on the buffers' capacity is settled.","title":"Real-time loops"},{"category":"section","location":"track.html#External-correlator-producers","page":"Track","text":"The Doppler estimator reads only each signal's correlator_outputs, never the samples, so an external producer (e.g. an FPGA streaming correlator dumps) can supply them and the host runs only the loops. (Transport, wire format and NCO marshaling live in GNSSReceiver.jl.)\n\nPer chunk:\n\nIngest. Build a CorrelatorOutput from the producer's raw accumulator and append it with append_correlator_output!, per signal in sample_index order:\nappend_correlator_output!(track_state, output, group, prn, sig)\nEstimate. Call estimate_dopplers_and_filter_prompt! with the per-band sampling frequencies (a NamedTuple or Dict keyed by get_band_id) in place of the samples:\nestimate_dopplers_and_filter_prompt!(track_state, (L1 = 25e6Hz, L5 = 25e6Hz))\n# or: estimate_dopplers_and_filter_prompt!(track_state, Dict(:L1 => 25e6Hz))\nBandMeasurements work too. The call consumes and clears each signal's correlator_outputs.\nMarshal the updated get_code_doppler / get_carrier_doppler back to the hardware NCOs.","title":"External correlator producers"},{"category":"section","location":"track.html#Caller-contract","page":"Track","text":"CorrelatorOutput.correlator: the raw accumulator (sum of products over integrated_samples, as normalize expects), e.g. built with EarlyPromptLateCorrelator / update_accumulator.\nCorrelatorOutput.integrated_samples: the producer's true sample count.\nCorrelatorOutput.sample_index: the end sample relative to the start of the current measurement, as the software path writes it (signal_start_sample returns to 1 each track!). A producer with a free-running global counter subtracts the measurement's origin; the estimate phase adds the band's TrackState sample_offsets before handing the record to the Doppler estimator.","title":"Caller contract"},{"category":"section","location":"track.html#Transport-delay","page":"Track","text":"The DLL discriminator takes the pre-update code_doppler as the one in effect during the integration, right for a one-chunk feedback pipeline. With a deeper delay, tag outputs with their epoch and schedule each NCO update for a known future epoch.","title":"Transport delay"},{"category":"section","location":"track.html#BandMeasurement","page":"Track","text":"One band's samples with their sampling_frequency and intermediate_frequency. A single band may pass a bare buffer, track!(buf, state, fs), or one BandMeasurement; several bands pass a NamedTuple keyed by GNSSSignals.get_band_id:\n\njulia> using Tracking, GNSSSignals\n\njulia> using Tracking: Hz\n\njulia> using Random: Xoshiro\n\njulia> track_state = TrackState(;\n           signals = (legacy_gps_l1 = (GPSL1CA(),), gps_l5 = (GPSL5I(),)),\n       );\n\njulia> track_state = add_satellite!(track_state; prn = 1, group = :legacy_gps_l1, code_phase = 0.0, carrier_doppler = 0.0Hz);\n\njulia> track_state = add_satellite!(track_state; prn = 1, group = :gps_l5,        code_phase = 0.0, carrier_doppler = 0.0Hz);\n\njulia> buf_l1 = randn(Xoshiro(1), ComplexF64, 4000);   # 1 ms at  4 MHz\n\njulia> buf_l5 = randn(Xoshiro(2), ComplexF64, 25000);  # 1 ms at 25 MHz\n\njulia> track!((L1 = BandMeasurement(buf_l1, 4e6Hz),\n               L5 = BandMeasurement(buf_l5, 25e6Hz)), track_state);\n\nNoise, not zeros: the default C/N₀ estimator measures the noise floor from the samples, and track! warns, once per signal, about an all-zero buffer (see AbstractNoiseEstimator). See Multi-band tracking for the full setup.","title":"BandMeasurement"},{"category":"section","location":"track.html#Downconversion-and-correlation","page":"Track","text":"The default CPUThreadedDownconvertAndCorrelator runs in Float32 and accepts any complex sample type. For Complex{Int16} samples there are three opt-in backends, each with a threaded and a single-threaded variant, selected via the downconvert_and_correlator keyword:\n\nInt16ThreadedDownconvertAndCorrelator / Int16DownconvertAndCorrelator: integer arithmetic, typically ~1.3–2.9× faster. Its required max_meas is the front end's full scale (the largest |real|/|imag|, e.g. 2^11 for a 12-bit ADC), which sizes the carrier replica against overflow; see Int16DownconvertAndCorrelator for why under-declaring it is catastrophic.\nOneBitThreadedDownconvertAndCorrelator / OneBitDownconvertAndCorrelator: measurement, carrier and code hard-limited to a sign bit, so downconversion is XOR and accumulation popcount; ~1.5–5.6× faster than Float32 (more at higher rates) for ≈2–3 dB of SNR. The ratio-normalised discriminators, C/N₀ and bit buffer do not mind the coarse amplitude. BPSK only.\nTwoBitThreadedDownconvertAndCorrelator / TwoBitDownconvertAndCorrelator: adds a magnitude bit to measurement and carrier ({±1, ±3}), recovering ≈2 dB of the one-bit loss (≈0.8 dB short of Float32) at about Int16 speed. threshold is the magnitude split in ADC counts (≈1σ of the input is near-optimal; the default 512 suits an AGC'd 12-bit capture). BPSK only.\n\nCorrelator sample shifts and the early/late spacing are documented in Correlator.","title":"Downconversion and correlation"},{"category":"function","location":"track.html#Tracking.track","page":"Track","text":"track(measurements, track_state; kwargs...)\n\n\nTrack one or more BandMeasurements: downconvert, correlate and estimate the Dopplers of every satellite, and return the updated TrackState with new phase/Doppler estimates and decoded bits.\n\nThe first argument is one of:\n\nArgument Meaning\nAbstractVecOrMat Bare sample buffer. Single-band TrackState only.\nBandMeasurement One band's bundled buffer + sample rate. Single-band TrackState.\nNamedTuple{...} of BandMeasurements Multi-band: one BandMeasurement per band id (see GNSSSignals.get_band_id).\n\nThe bare buffer takes sampling_frequency as the next positional argument and an intermediate_frequency keyword (default 0). Every form runs the same per-chunk loop: downconvert and correlate all groups, then estimate.\n\nThe returned TrackState is structurally detached from the input: each group's key set and slot vector are copied, so adding, removing or tracking satellites on one never affects the other. The copy is shallow, though, and these are shared with the input (treat it as a stale handle, or deepcopy it first):\n\nthe per-satellite scratch vectors (filtered_prompts, correlator_outputs, the soft bits, the C/N₀ estimator's buffer), overwritten by the next call on either state; a bare downconvert_and_correlate likewise appends to the input's correlator_outputs;\neach signal's noise estimator, which track advances in place: branched states divide by one noise reference, and advancing two concurrently races, so build one TrackState per thread (see downconvert_and_correlate for why the window is not copied);\nthe doppler_estimator itself, so results can be read off the instance you passed in. One with state of its own (TrackingLoops' VectorPLLAndDLL) is advanced by track as by track!, and two branches would mix their records in one navigation filter.\n\nFor real-time loops, construct the correlator once outside the loop:\n\ndc = CPUThreadedDownconvertAndCorrelator()\nwhile got_chunk(rx)\n    chunk = read_chunk!(rx)\n    track_state =\n        track(chunk, track_state, sampling_frequency; downconvert_and_correlator = dc)\nend\n\nThe default builds fresh per-thread scratch buffers on every call. See track! for the in-place variant.\n\nThe coherent-integration length is set per signal with set_preferred_num_code_blocks_to_integrate!, not passed to track:\n\nset_preferred_num_code_blocks_to_integrate!(track_state, :gps_l5, 1, GPSL5I, 10)  # PRN 1 L5I: 10 ms\n\nThe conventional estimator caps the carrier loop bandwidth at 0.09 / T_int and the code loop's at 0.018 / T_int, so longer integration stays stable (see ConventionalPLLAndDLL).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.track"},{"category":"function","location":"track.html#Tracking.track!","page":"Track","text":"track!(\n    measurements,\n    track_state;\n    downconvert_and_correlator,\n    doppler_update_interval\n)\n\n\nIn-place version of track: overwrites the satellite slots of track_state and returns it.\n\nAfter one warmup call the single-threaded path is allocation-free. The threaded path (CPUThreadedDownconvertAndCorrelator) keeps a residual of about 96 B per group per correlate pass (one per doppler_update_interval chunk, so it scales with the buffer length), when running on more than one thread with more than one work item in the group (the noise despreads count, riding the first group); see CPUThreadedDownconvertAndCorrelator for why.\n\nFor real-time loops, construct the correlator once outside the loop:\n\ntrack_state = TrackState(signal, initial_sats)\ndc = CPUThreadedDownconvertAndCorrelator()      # hoist!\n\nwhile got_chunk(rx)\n    chunk = read_chunk!(rx)\n    track!(chunk, track_state, sampling_frequency; downconvert_and_correlator = dc)\nend\n\nIts per-thread scratch buffers grow on first use; the default would regrow them every call.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.track!"},{"category":"function","location":"track.html#Tracking.set_preferred_num_code_blocks_to_integrate!","page":"Track","text":"set_preferred_num_code_blocks_to_integrate!(\n    track_state,\n    group,\n    sat_id,\n    sig,\n    num_code_blocks\n)\n\n\nSet the preferred coherent-integration length, in primary code blocks, of one signal on one satellite (the addressed TrackedSignal's preferred_num_code_blocks_to_integrate). The actual length is capped by the bit/secondary-code period and held at 1 until sync (see calc_num_code_blocks_to_integrate); the conventional estimator caps its loop bandwidths against the integration time, so the loop stays stable.\n\nFor data-bearing signals the length must divide the code blocks per bit (20 for GPS L1 C/A, 10 for GPS L5I), or an ArgumentError is thrown (#128). Pilots accept any length of at least one block.\n\nAddressed like the per-signal accessors; the per-signal form names the group even on a single-group TrackState:\n\nset_preferred_num_code_blocks_to_integrate!(ts, :gps_l5, 1, GPSL5I, 10)  # (group, prn, signal)\nset_preferred_num_code_blocks_to_integrate!(ts, :gps_l5, 1, 10)          # single-signal sat\nset_preferred_num_code_blocks_to_integrate!(ts, 1, 10)                   # single-group state\nset_preferred_num_code_blocks_to_integrate!(ts, 10)                      # 1 group, 1 sat, 1 signal\n\nMutates track_state in place and returns it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.set_preferred_num_code_blocks_to_integrate!"},{"category":"type","location":"track.html#Tracking.BandMeasurement","page":"Track","text":"One band's sample buffer with the sampling_frequency and intermediate_frequency it was captured at.\n\nFields:\n\nsamples::S: complex sample buffer (Vector for one antenna, Matrix with rows = samples and columns = antennas for an antenna array). Must be dense (unit row stride, columns packed back-to-back), as the SIMD kernels read it through raw pointers; anything else is an ArgumentError. Contiguous views (e.g. view(buf, 1:4000)) are fine.\nsampling_frequency::F: the buffer's sample rate (e.g. 4e6Hz)\nintermediate_frequency::F: the band's IF (defaults to 0.0Hz)\n\nA multi-band track takes a NamedTuple of them keyed by band; a single band may also pass a plain buffer and sample rate.\n\nBandMeasurement(buf, 4e6Hz)                              # IF defaults to 0.0Hz\nBandMeasurement(buf, 4e6Hz, 1.575e6Hz)                   # explicit IF\nBandMeasurement(buf; sampling_frequency = 4e6Hz)         # kwarg form\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.BandMeasurement"},{"category":"type","location":"track.html#Tracking.BandMeasurements","page":"Track","text":"A NamedTuple of BandMeasurements, the multi-band input of track / track!, keyed by GNSSSignals.get_band_id (e.g. :L1, :L5), a compile-time constant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.BandMeasurements"},{"category":"type","location":"track.html#Tracking.CPUDownconvertAndCorrelator","page":"Track","text":"CPU-based implementation of downconversion and correlation. Its scratch buffers grow lazily on first use and are reused thereafter, so a hoisted instance is allocation-free per track! call in steady state.\n\nFor real-time loops, construct the correlator once outside the track! loop and pass it via the downconvert_and_correlator keyword argument; the default value rebuilds the buffers on every call.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.CPUDownconvertAndCorrelator"},{"category":"type","location":"track.html#Tracking.CPUThreadedDownconvertAndCorrelator","page":"Track","text":"Multi-threaded CPU downconvert and correlate, parallelized over the satellites (PRNs) of each group. Holds one set of scratch buffers per thread, indexed by Threads.threadid() inside @batch (which pins each iteration to a thread), grown lazily and reused thereafter.\n\nWith more than one thread and more than one work item, each @batch launch (one per downconvert_and_correlate! call, so one per code block inside track!) keeps a Polyester allocation of ~96 B. It is bounded per launch and does not scale with the input buffer. Measuring a noise density keeps it at ~96 B (the noise despreads ride the loop as extra items behind one pointer, see _park_noise_items!), but a single-satellite state then pays it too, as its loop has two items; in exchange the despread usually costs a fraction of its ~1.75 µs serial time.\n\nPolyester copies everything the @batch region references into one argument tuple per launch. A tuple of only isbits members (bare Arrays qualify, as PtrArrays) is elided; otherwise it is heap-allocated at its sizeof, with immutable structs copied by value and mutable objects as one pointer. The non-isbits member here is the GNSS signal (GPSL1CA wraps a Matrix{Int16} and a non-isbits SignalLUT), which code-replica generation needs. A prototype that generates from the LUT's bare arrays passed as separate arguments measures 0 B/launch at full throughput, but needs a GNSSSignals API that does not re-wrap them in a struct; a serial code-gen pre-pass also reaches 0 but serializes ~30 % of the work. Neither is worth ~96 B per launch.\n\nFor real-time loops, construct the correlator once outside the track! loop and pass it via the downconvert_and_correlator keyword argument; the default value rebuilds the per-thread buffers on every call.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.CPUThreadedDownconvertAndCorrelator"},{"category":"type","location":"track.html#Tracking.Int16DownconvertAndCorrelator","page":"Track","text":"Integer (Complex{Int16}) hybrid-blocked CPU downconvert + correlate backend (single-threaded). Opt-in alternative to [CPUDownconvertAndCorrelator] for Complex{Int16} sample buffers; errors on any other sample element type. Construct it once outside the track! loop and pass it via the downconvert_and_correlator keyword: allocation-free in steady state for SVector sample shifts (EPL/VEPL); runtime AbstractVector shifts allocate only the returned Vector.\n\nArguments\n\nmax_meas (required, no default) is the largest |real|/|imag| any sample will take, i.e. the front end's full-scale (e.g. 2^11 for a 12-bit ADC). The carrier amplitude is the largest with 2·max_meas·amplitude ≤ typemax(Int16), since the complex wipe DI = mᵣ·cos + mᵢ·sin sums two products. Under-declaring max_meas silently overflows the Int16 wipe and corrupts the correlation; over-declaring is safe and only coarsens the carrier quantisation.\n\nnote: Performance: keep `max_meas < 2^14`\nFor max_meas ≥ 2^14 no Int16-safe amplitude ≥ 1 exists: the backend falls back to an exact Int32 wipe (off the vpmaddwd fast path) and a shorter strip-mine block. Correct, but measurably slower.\n\nblk is the strip-mine block length in samples. It must be ≥ 1 (blk ≤ 0 would hang track!, #169); a larger-than-safe blk is clamped so the accumulators never wrap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.Int16DownconvertAndCorrelator"},{"category":"type","location":"track.html#Tracking.Int16ThreadedDownconvertAndCorrelator","page":"Track","text":"Multi-threaded Int16DownconvertAndCorrelator: one Int16ScratchBuffers per thread, the immutable carrier table shared.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.Int16ThreadedDownconvertAndCorrelator"},{"category":"type","location":"track.html#Tracking.OneBitDownconvertAndCorrelator","page":"Track","text":"One-bit (hard-limited) bit-wise CPU downconvert + correlate backend (single-threaded). Opt-in alternative to [CPUDownconvertAndCorrelator] for Complex{Int16} sample buffers: it 1-bit-quantises measurement, carrier and code and correlates with XOR + popcount, trading ≈2–3 dB of SNR for bit-wise speed. Construct it once outside the track! loop and pass it via the downconvert_and_correlator keyword for an allocation-free steady state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.OneBitDownconvertAndCorrelator"},{"category":"type","location":"track.html#Tracking.OneBitThreadedDownconvertAndCorrelator","page":"Track","text":"Multi-threaded OneBitDownconvertAndCorrelator, one OneBitScratchBuffers per thread.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.OneBitThreadedDownconvertAndCorrelator"},{"category":"type","location":"track.html#Tracking.TwoBitDownconvertAndCorrelator","page":"Track","text":"Two-bit (sign + magnitude) bit-wise CPU downconvert + correlate backend (single-threaded). Opt-in alternative to [CPUDownconvertAndCorrelator] for Complex{Int16} sample buffers, between OneBitDownconvertAndCorrelator and Int16DownconvertAndCorrelator: measurement and carrier are 4-level {±1, ±3}, the code 1-bit, correlated with XOR + masked popcount. Recovers ≈2 dB of correlation SNR over the one-bit backend (≈0.8 dB total quantisation loss vs Float32) at bit-wise speed. Construct it once outside the track! loop and pass it via the downconvert_and_correlator keyword for an allocation-free steady state.\n\nthreshold is the measurement magnitude split in ADC counts (|component| ≥ threshold ⇒ ±3); near 1σ of the front end's input is the near-optimal 4-level quantiser.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.TwoBitDownconvertAndCorrelator"},{"category":"type","location":"track.html#Tracking.TwoBitThreadedDownconvertAndCorrelator","page":"Track","text":"Multi-threaded TwoBitDownconvertAndCorrelator, one TwoBitScratchBuffers per thread.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.TwoBitThreadedDownconvertAndCorrelator"},{"category":"type","location":"track.html#Tracking.AbstractDownconvertAndCorrelator","page":"Track","text":"Supertype of the downconvert-and-correlate backends.\n\nThe downconvert_and_correlate(!) loops are defined once on this type (see downconvert_and_correlate_cpu.jl); a subtype implements the hooks: _despread_one_signal! (the correlation primitive, for satellites and the noise reference alike; alone it gives a working single-signal backend), _correlate_signals / _scratch_buffers (multi-signal kernel and scratch), _threading (default serial) and _check_sample_type (default no-op).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.AbstractDownconvertAndCorrelator"},{"category":"section","location":"bit_sync.html#Bit-and-Secondary-Code-Sync","page":"Bit and Secondary-Code Sync","text":"Until a signal's bit boundary (or, for a pilot, its secondary-code phase) is found it integrates one primary code period at a time. Sync unlocks longer coherent integrations (e.g. 20 ms for GPS L1 C/A), decoded bits, and the code phase modulo a whole symbol, which a pseudorange needs.\n\nUsers only need has_bit_or_secondary_code_been_found per signal (see the per-signal accessor table) to gate get_soft_bits. The detectors and the bit buffer are TrackingLoops'; see BitBuffer.","title":"Bit and Secondary-Code Sync"},{"category":"section","location":"bit_sync.html#Code-phase-wrap-period","page":"Bit and Secondary-Code Sync","text":"The shared TrackedSat.code_phase wraps at the least common multiple of the signals' wraps (#129), each of which depends on the signal's sync state:\n\nBefore sync: its primary code length, since the bit or secondary chip of the current period is unknown.\nAfter sync: one symbol period, primary × secondary_code_length for a pilot and primary × blocks_per_data_bit for a data signal.\n\nSat tracks Before sync After full sync\nGPS L1 C/A only 1023 1023 × 20 = 20460 (one 50 Hz data bit)\nGPS L5I only 10230 10230 × 10 = 102300 (one NH10 cycle / one data bit)\nGPS L1C-P only 10230 10230 × 1800 ≈ 18.4 M (one overlay-code cycle, ≈ 18 s)\nL1C-P + L1C-D + L1CA 10230 18.4 M (L1C-P dominates)\n\nmod(code_phase, primary) is a signal's replica phase, div(code_phase, primary) its position within the symbol. max_code_length is the post-sync bound, current_code_wrap the runtime wrap.","title":"Code-phase wrap period"},{"category":"section","location":"bit_sync.html#Sync-detection","page":"Bit and Secondary-Code Sync","text":"Each signal's detector (detect_bit_or_secondary_code_sync) comes in one of three families:\n\nSoft, maximum-energy CFAR: GPS L1 C/A's bit edge and every short secondary code (1 < N ≤ 100). It locks when the peak hypothesis beats its runner-up at the confidence get_bit_edge_detection_confidence (default 0.999), needs two periods first, and fires on a period boundary, so it reports phase = 0.\nHard rotation sweep: the 1800-chip overlay pilots GPS L1C-P and BeiDou B1C-P. It matches the prompt signs against the overlay at every rotation within get_bit_edge_or_secondary_code_tolerance, locks after one period at any alignment and reports the recovered chip phase.\nTrivial: one symbol per primary period (Galileo E1B, E6-B, GPS L1C-D, L2CM, BeiDou B2b-I, B1C-D), and Galileo E5a-QP (no data, no overlay), sync on the first block. GPS L2CL, a pilot without secondary code, never syncs and stays pre-sync.\n\nMin-to-fire is the number of blocks before a detector can lock.\n\nSignal Detector Min-to-fire Accept rule Phase Blocks per symbol\nGPS L1 C/A soft bit-edge CFAR 40 blocks (2 × 20) confidence 0.999 0 20\nGalileo E1B trivial n/a always 0 1\nGPS L5I soft secondary CFAR 20 blocks (2 × NH10) confidence 0.999 0 10\nGPS L5Q soft secondary CFAR 40 blocks (2 × NH20) confidence 0.999 0 20 (pilot)\nGPS L1C-D trivial n/a always 0 1\nGPS L1C-P hard rotation sweep 1800 blocks 45 errors (2.5 %) 0..1799 n/a (pilot)\nGPS L2CM trivial n/a always 0 1\nGPS L2CL none (dataless pilot) n/a n/a 0 n/a (pilot)\nGalileo E1C soft secondary CFAR 50 blocks (2 × CS25) confidence 0.999 0 25 (pilot)\nGalileo E5a-I soft secondary CFAR 40 blocks (2 × CS20) confidence 0.999 0 20\nGalileo E5a-Q soft secondary CFAR 200 blocks (2 × CS100) confidence 0.999 0 100 (pilot)\nGalileo E5b-I soft secondary CFAR 8 blocks (2 × CS4) confidence 0.999 0 4\nGalileo E5b-Q soft secondary CFAR 200 blocks (2 × CS100) confidence 0.999 0 100 (pilot)\nGalileo E5a-QP trivial n/a always 0 n/a (pilot)\nGalileo E6-B trivial n/a always 0 1\nGalileo E6-C soft secondary CFAR 200 blocks (2 × CS100) confidence 0.999 0 100 (pilot)\nBeiDou B1I soft secondary CFAR 40 blocks (2 × NH20) confidence 0.999 0 20\nBeiDou B3I soft secondary CFAR 40 blocks (2 × NH20) confidence 0.999 0 20\nBeiDou B2b-I trivial n/a always 0 1\nBeiDou B2a data soft secondary CFAR 10 blocks (2 × 5) confidence 0.999 0 5\nBeiDou B2a pilot soft secondary CFAR 200 blocks (2 × 100) confidence 0.999 0 100 (pilot)\nBeiDou B1C data trivial n/a always 0 1\nBeiDou B1C pilot hard rotation sweep 1800 blocks 45 errors (2.5 %) 0..1799 n/a (pilot)\n\nBeiDou B1I/B3I GEO satellites (PRN 1-5, 59-63) carry no NH20 overlay and broadcast D2, so the detector never locks on them: they track and range but stay pre-sync (see Known limitations).\n\nThe confidence (soft detectors) and the tolerance (hard sweep only) can be overridden per signal; the hard sweep reads its tolerance on every call, so no TrackState rebuild is needed:\n\njulia> using Tracking, TrackingLoops, GNSSSignals\n\njulia> TrackingLoops.get_bit_edge_or_secondary_code_tolerance(GPSL1C_P())  # default\n0.025\n\njulia> # Loosen the L1C-P ceiling to 5 % (= 90 errors over 1800 blocks) for low-C/N₀ work.\n       TrackingLoops.get_bit_edge_or_secondary_code_tolerance(::GPSL1C_P) = 0.05;\n\njulia> TrackingLoops.get_bit_edge_or_secondary_code_tolerance(GPSL1C_P())  # after override\n0.05","title":"Sync detection"},{"category":"section","location":"bit_sync.html#Code-phase-seeding","page":"Bit and Secondary-Code Sync","text":"When a signal with a secondary code syncs, its secondary_phase snaps TrackedSat.code_phase into the right secondary-code window, by a multiple of the primary length; the within-period phase is kept (#117). Of several synced signals, the one with the longest primary × secondary length wins.\n\nA signal without secondary code (e.g. GPS L1 C/A) needs no snap: its detector locks at a true bit boundary, so the widened wrap starts at the bit's first period, and the bit buffer's block counter, which reset preserves, keeps the bit cadence across track! calls.","title":"Code-phase seeding"},{"category":"function","location":"bit_sync.html#Tracking.max_code_length","page":"Bit and Secondary-Code Sync","text":"max_code_length(signals)\n\n\nUpper bound on the shared sat.code_phase wrap period, in chips: the least common multiple of the per-signal wraps once every signal has synced (GPS L1 C/A: 1023 × 20 = 20460, one data bit; L1C-P: 10230 × 1800, one secondary-code cycle). Folds to a literal for a concrete signals type.\n\nThe runtime wrap, current_code_wrap, is shorter for unsynced signals.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.max_code_length"},{"category":"function","location":"bit_sync.html#Tracking.current_code_wrap","page":"Bit and Secondary-Code Sync","text":"current_code_wrap(signals)\n\n\nThe runtime wrap period of the shared sat.code_phase, in chips: like max_code_length, but a signal not yet synced (bit_buffer.found false) contributes only its primary code length, since its bit or secondary chip is unknown. The least common multiple keeps the wrap a multiple of every signal's replica wrap (#129).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.current_code_wrap"},{"category":"section","location":"correlator.html#Correlator","page":"Correlator","text":"The correlator correlates the incoming signal with the local code replica at several code-phase offsets (early, prompt, late, …). Each TrackedSignal holds its own correlator, so a multi-signal satellite carries one per signal.","title":"Correlator"},{"category":"section","location":"correlator.html#Why-early/late-spacing-matters","page":"Correlator","text":"The PLL discriminator and C/N₀ estimator consume the prompt; the early and late accumulators give the DLL the slope of the code-correlation triangle. Early minus late crosses zero when the replica is aligned, and its sign tells the DLL which way to push.\n\nNotation: d is the early-to-late spacing in chips (d = 0.5: E and L ½ chip apart), ε the code-phase error in chips, B_fe the front-end (analog + ADC) bandwidth, T_chip = 1 / f_code the chip period, f_code the chip rate (1.023 MHz for GPS L1 C/A) and Fs the sampling frequency.\n\nThermal-noise jitter decreases as d shrinks, because the noise on E and L is correlated and partially cancels in their difference (Van Dierendonck, Fenton & Ford, \"Theory and Performance of Narrow Correlator Spacing in a GPS Receiver\", NAVIGATION 39(3), 1992). A wider spacing only smooths a time-multiplexed (dither) DLL; Tracking.jl computes E, P and L simultaneously from the same samples.\nMultipath error also decreases as d shrinks, as the discriminator samples the correlation function nearer the peak (same paper).\nRobustness to dynamics decreases as d shrinks. The discriminator is linear only over |ε| < d/2 (the pull-in range T_DLL, Springer Handbook of GNSS, §14.4.4); beyond it the slope drops to zero with the sign still correct. The lock rule of thumb 3σ_DLL + ε_D ≤ T_DLL ≈ d/2 (eq. 14.57) leaves a narrow d less budget for dynamic stress error ε_D, which is why NovAtel's narrow-correlator design widens d during acquisition and narrows it once locked.\nFront-end bandwidth caps the gains: below roughly d ≈ 1 / (B_fe · T_chip) the band-limited peak is rounded, the slope flattens and jitter grows again. Van Dierendonck reports d ≈ 0.1 as optimal at 8 MHz on L1 C/A.","title":"Why early/late spacing matters"},{"category":"section","location":"correlator.html#The-sample-rate-sets-the-minimum-d","page":"Correlator","text":"preferred_early_late_to_prompt_code_shift is the early-to-prompt (and prompt-to-late) shift, so d is twice it. It is rounded to the nearest whole sample shift, at least one, so the realized spacing is d = 2 · max(1, round(shift · Fs / f_code)) · f_code / Fs chips. A d ≈ 0.1 on L1 C/A needs Fs ≥ 20 · f_code ≈ 20.46 MHz; at 4 MHz d saturates at 2 · 1.023e6 / 4e6 ≈ 0.51 chips whatever the preferred value.\n\nThe VeryEarlyPromptLateCorrelator adds a very-early / very-late pair for the multi-peaked correlation function of BOC signals like Galileo E1B: the inner pair locks the central peak, the outer pair disambiguates it from the side peaks.","title":"The sample rate sets the minimum d"},{"category":"section","location":"correlator.html#Default-Correlators","page":"Correlator","text":"get_default_correlator returns, per signal type:\n\nVeryEarlyPromptLateCorrelator for the BOC-family signals GPS L1C-D, GPS L1C-P, Galileo E1B, Galileo E1C and BeiDou B1C;\nEarlyPromptLateCorrelator for every other signal.","title":"Default Correlators"},{"category":"section","location":"correlator.html#Correlator-Types","page":"Correlator","text":"In the TrackingLoops manual:\n\nEarlyPromptLateCorrelator\nVeryEarlyPromptLateCorrelator","title":"Correlator Types"},{"category":"section","location":"correlator.html#Accessing-Correlator-Values","page":"Correlator","text":"In the TrackingLoops manual:\n\nget_early\nget_prompt\nget_late\nget_accumulators\nget_num_accumulators\nget_num_ants(::AbstractCorrelator)","title":"Accessing Correlator Values"},{"category":"section","location":"correlator.html#Correlator-outputs","page":"Correlator","text":"Each completed integration within a processing chunk is recorded as a CorrelatorOutput, collected per signal and consumed by the Doppler estimator after the chunk (see Chunked Doppler updates). An external correlator producer (e.g. an FPGA) can build these itself and feed them straight to the estimator — see External correlator producers.\n\nIn the TrackingLoops manual:\n\nCorrelatorOutput","title":"Correlator outputs"},{"category":"section","location":"correlator.html#Sample-shifts","page":"Correlator","text":"get_correlator_sample_shifts places each accumulator relative to the prompt, in samples; get_early_late_sample_spacing, the spacing of the outermost pair, scales the DLL discriminator back to chips.\n\nIn the TrackingLoops manual:\n\nget_correlator_sample_shifts\nget_early_late_sample_spacing\nupdate_accumulator(::EarlyPromptLateCorrelator, ::Any)\nupdate_accumulator(::VeryEarlyPromptLateCorrelator, ::Any)","title":"Sample shifts"},{"category":"section","location":"correlator.html#Antenna-and-accumulator-counts","page":"Correlator","text":"In the TrackingLoops manual:\n\nNumAnts\nNumAccumulators","title":"Antenna and accumulator counts"},{"category":"section","location":"correlator.html#Post-correlation-filter","page":"Correlator","text":"A post-correlation filter reduces the per-antenna taps to the single channel the Doppler and C/N₀ estimators see. It declares its combining weights through get_weights and Tracking applies them, so the measured noise covariance is reduced through the same weights and the C/N₀'s numerator and denominator describe one channel (see CN0 Estimator).\n\nDefaultPostCorrFilter is the identity for a single antenna and selects the last element for an array. For an array a beamformer is the natural override; see Tracking State.\n\nIn the TrackingLoops manual:\n\nAbstractPostCorrFilter\nDefaultPostCorrFilter\nupdate(::DefaultPostCorrFilter, ::Any)\nget_weights","title":"Post-correlation filter"},{"category":"section","location":"correlator.html#Integration-sizing","page":"Correlator","text":"","title":"Integration sizing"},{"category":"section","location":"correlator.html#Adjusting-the-spacing-of-the-stock-correlator","page":"Correlator","text":"Changing d needs no custom correlator: pass d / 2 in chips as preferred_early_late_to_prompt_code_shift (here d = 0.2):\n\njulia> using Tracking, TrackingLoops, GNSSSignals\n\njulia> using Tracking: Hz\n\njulia> track_state = TrackState(; signal = GPSL1CA());\n\njulia> sat = TrackedSat(GPSL1CA(), 1, 50.0, 1000.0Hz;\n                        correlator = EarlyPromptLateCorrelator(\n                            preferred_early_late_to_prompt_code_shift = 0.1,\n                        ));\n\njulia> track_state = add_satellite!(track_state, :default, sat);\n\njulia> get_correlator(track_state, :default, 1, 1).preferred_early_late_to_prompt_code_shift\n0.1\n\nVeryEarlyPromptLateCorrelator exposes the inner (preferred_early_late_to_prompt_code_shift, default 0.15) and the outer (preferred_very_early_late_to_prompt_code_shift, default 0.6) shift. Either is subject to the sample-rate floor above.","title":"Adjusting the spacing of the stock correlator"},{"category":"section","location":"correlator.html#Changing-the-spacing-of-a-satellite-that's-already-tracking","page":"Correlator","text":"The sat, signal and correlator are immutable, so build a replacement sat with Accessors.@set and write it into the slot with Dictionaries.set!:\n\njulia> using Accessors, Dictionaries\n\njulia> sat     = track_state.groups[:default].satellites[1];\n\njulia> new_sat = @set sat.signals[1].correlator.preferred_early_late_to_prompt_code_shift = 0.7;\n\njulia> Dictionaries.set!(track_state.groups[:default].satellites, 1, new_sat);\n\njulia> get_correlator(track_state, :default, 1, 1).preferred_early_late_to_prompt_code_shift\n0.7\n\n@set keeps everything else, including the in-flight accumulator, and the TrackState's concrete type does not change.","title":"Changing the spacing of a satellite that's already tracking"},{"category":"section","location":"correlator.html#Custom-Correlators","page":"Correlator","text":"Write a correlator for a layout the stock types lack: extra taps, asymmetric shifts, or a different prompt index. Subtype AbstractCorrelator (or AbstractEarlyPromptLateCorrelator to inherit the early/late accessors) and implement:\n\nMethod Purpose\nget_accumulators(c) Return the backing storage of accumulators (e.g. an SVector of ComplexF64).\nget_num_accumulators(c) Return how many accumulators the correlator holds. Defaults to size(get_accumulators(c), 1) — only override if your layout differs.\nupdate_accumulator(c, accumulators) Construct a fresh correlator instance with the given accumulators (immutable update).\nget_correlator_sample_shifts(c, sampling_frequency, code_frequency) Return an SVector{N,Int} of sample offsets relative to the prompt, ordered from latest replica to earliest.\n\nget_prompt_index defaults to the middle accumulator; override it for an asymmetric layout.","title":"Custom Correlators"},{"category":"section","location":"correlator.html#Skeleton","page":"Correlator","text":"A 7-tap correlator, e.g. for a multipath-mitigation discriminator that fits a slope over several early/late taps:\n\njulia> using Tracking, TrackingLoops, GNSSSignals, StaticArrays\n\njulia> using TrackingLoops:\n           AbstractEarlyPromptLateCorrelator,\n           get_initial_accumulator,\n           calc_preferred_code_shift_to_sample_shift,\n           NumAnts,\n           NumAccumulators\n\njulia> struct MultiTapCorrelator{M,T} <: AbstractEarlyPromptLateCorrelator{M}\n           accumulators::SVector{7,T}\n           tap_spacing_chips::Float64\n           # Pin M from the accumulator element type, as TrackingLoops' EPL does.\n           function MultiTapCorrelator(\n               accumulators::AbstractVector{SVector{M,T}}, tap_spacing_chips,\n           ) where {M,T<:Complex}\n               new{M,SVector{M,T}}(accumulators, tap_spacing_chips)\n           end\n           function MultiTapCorrelator(\n               accumulators::AbstractVector{T}, tap_spacing_chips,\n           ) where {T<:Complex}\n               new{1,T}(accumulators, tap_spacing_chips)\n           end\n       end\n\njulia> # Keyword constructor, like the EPL one\n       function MultiTapCorrelator(; num_ants::NumAnts = NumAnts(1),\n                                     tap_spacing_chips = 0.1)\n           MultiTapCorrelator(\n               get_initial_accumulator(num_ants, NumAccumulators(7)),\n               tap_spacing_chips,\n           )\n       end;\n\njulia> TrackingLoops.update_accumulator(c::MultiTapCorrelator, accumulators) =\n           MultiTapCorrelator(accumulators, c.tap_spacing_chips);\n\njulia> function TrackingLoops.get_correlator_sample_shifts(\n           c::MultiTapCorrelator, sampling_frequency, code_frequency,\n       )\n           s = calc_preferred_code_shift_to_sample_shift(\n               c.tap_spacing_chips, sampling_frequency, code_frequency,\n           )\n           SVector(-3s, -2s, -s, 0, s, 2s, 3s)\n       end;\n\nBuild the TrackState from a sat carrying it, so the slot type is the custom correlator's:\n\njulia> using Tracking: Hz\n\njulia> sat = TrackedSat(GPSL1CA(), 1, 50.0, 1000.0Hz;\n                        correlator = MultiTapCorrelator(tap_spacing_chips = 0.1));\n\njulia> track_state = TrackState(GPSL1CA(), sat);\n\njulia> get_num_accumulators(get_correlator(track_state, 1))\n7\n\nThe default DLL discriminator reads only the outermost early/late pair, so pair a multi-tap correlator with a Custom Doppler Estimator. TrackingLoops' src/correlators/early_prompt_late.jl and very_early_prompt_late.jl show the full pattern, including multi-antenna accumulators.","title":"Skeleton"},{"category":"function","location":"correlator.html#Tracking.get_correlator_outputs","page":"Correlator","text":"get_correlator_outputs(t)\n\n\nThe CorrelatorOutputs this signal completed in the current chunk, in order. The Doppler estimator consumes and clears them, so read them inside a custom estimator or right after a bare downconvert_and_correlate!. See Chunked Doppler updates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.get_correlator_outputs"},{"category":"function","location":"correlator.html#Tracking.append_correlator_output!","page":"Correlator","text":"append_correlator_output!(t, output)\n\n\nAppend an externally built CorrelatorOutput to signal's per-chunk buffer and return signal: the ingest path for an external correlator producer (e.g. FPGA dumps). Append per signal in sample_index order, then run estimate_dopplers_and_filter_prompt!, which folds and clears the buffer. Type-checked against the signal's correlator, unlike pushing to get_correlator_outputs. See External correlator producers.\n\n\n\n\n\nappend_correlator_output!(s, output, id)\n\n\nAppend an externally built CorrelatorOutput to the addressed signal's buffer and return track_state. Addressed like the per-signal accessors (e.g. get_correlator_outputs):\n\nappend_correlator_output!(track_state, output) — one group, one sat, one signal.\nappend_correlator_output!(track_state, output, prn) — one group, one signal.\nappend_correlator_output!(track_state, output, group, prn) — multi-group, one signal.\nappend_correlator_output!(track_state, output, group, prn, sig) — per-signal.\n\nThe signal-level method (append_correlator_output!(::TrackedSignal, output)) carries the contract; see it and External correlator producers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.append_correlator_output!"},{"category":"function","location":"correlator.html#Tracking.calc_signal_samples_to_integrate","page":"Correlator","text":"calc_signal_samples_to_integrate(\n    signal,\n    signal_start_sample,\n    sampling_frequency,\n    code_doppler,\n    code_phase,\n    preferred_num_code_blocks_to_integrate,\n    secondary_code_or_bit_found,\n    num_samples_signal\n)\n\n\nReturn (samples_to_integrate, is_integration_completed): the samples up to the end of the integration's code blocks, or of the signal if that comes first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tracking.calc_signal_samples_to_integrate"}]
}
